{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '150,720p'\"","aggregated_output":"   150\t  sharing the socket does not share the cookie gate. Section 5.9 stages\n   151\t  the exposure as the DHT's own slices did.\n   152\t\n   153\t**Implementation gaps.**\n   154\t\n   155\t- G1. A loaded index does not survive a restart: the node's loads are\n   156\t  process state (`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc`, `:loads`).\n   157\t- G5. A load record has no owner. `:loads` is keyed by address alone\n   158\t  (`dao/space/dht.cljc:1166-1170`), `forget` removes the one shared\n   159\t  record (`1204-1211`), and `yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots`\n   160\t  (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc:153-160`) folds every loaded index\n   161\t  whoever loaded it and why. One consumer today hides this; a follower\n   162\t  is a second. Section 5.5 gives loads holders.\n   163\t- G2. A name whose resolved address moved under a live session is neither\n   164\t  relinked nor reported; a repeat `(require ...)` is silent.\n   165\t- G3. `announce!` (`dao/space/dht.cljc:387-393`) is unsigned, private and\n   166\t  discarded by `close!`. That is correct for what it is, the node's work\n   167\t  queue (section 5.4), and is no defect.\n   168\t- G4. The status lines of `dao.stream.remote.md` and `dao.stream.ws.md`\n   169\t  say \"design target, not implemented\" while `dao.stream.remote`,\n   170\t  `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.stream.ws-project` are in `src/cljc`. Doc\n   171\t  drift; H1 corrects the first.\n   172\t\n   173\t**Intentionally deferred, and still deferred:** section 11.\n   174\t\n   175\t## 5. The design\n   176\t\n   177\t### 5.1 Medium (decision 1)\n   178\t\n   179\t**The head trace lives on a ring the publisher alone writes, which its\n   180\tnode serves as one mirror-table entry on the datagram socket it already\n   181\thas.**\n   182\t\n   183\t- The **head board** of a principal is a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` of\n   184\t  capacity 1 (section 5.3), created by the node's composition. It is an\n   185\t  ordinary table entry under the ring's own identity, the random\n   186\t  identity every ring is created with, and its surface is `#{:reader}`.\n   187\t- The channel is `dao.stream.udp` on the node's one socket.\n   188\t  `dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6 already lets `dao.jing.dht` and\n   189\t  `dao.stream.udp` share it: the DHT owns the maps carrying\n   190\t  `:dao.jing.dht/v`, the UDP channel every other decodable value. One\n   191\t  address, one NAT mapping, both interpreters, neither aware of the other.\n   192\t- Any other channel `dao.stream.remote.md` section 3 defines (WebSocket,\n   193\t  a relay pair) carries the same entry unchanged. The plain API takes\n   194\t  channel descriptors; only the token form presumes UDP.\n   195\t\n   196\t**A name is looked up; it is never an identity (C3).**\n   197\t\n   198\t- The **board name** of a principal is the string `\"yin.head/\"`\n   199\t  followed by the principal id of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.2. It is\n   200\t  lookup data. It appears in no descriptor, no cursor and no\n   201\t  `:dao.stream/identity`.\n   202\t- A peer's composition holds, beside its table, a **name map**\n   203\t  `{name identity}`: plain data it owns, each value the key of an entry\n   204\t  in its table.\n   205\t- One request shape is added to `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.1. It carries\n   206\t  `:dao.stream.remote/name n` in place of `:dao.stream/identity`, and\n   207\t  its op is `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the only op a name may ride:\n   208\t\n   209\t  ```clojure\n   210\t  {:dao.stream.remote/name \"yin.head/ed25519:<hex>\"\n   211\t   :dao.stream.remote/op   :dao.stream/descriptor\n   212\t   :dao.stream.remote/args []\n   213\t   :dao.stream.remote/id   n}\n   214\t  ```\n   215\t\n   216\t- The mirror answers it with exactly the `descriptor` answer of the\n   217\t  entry the name maps to, carrying that entry's true\n   218\t  `:dao.stream/identity` and surface, and the name echoed under\n   219\t  `:dao.stream.remote/name`. A name the map lacks, or one mapped to an\n   220\t  identity the table lacks, is the `not-found` protocol error with the\n   221\t  name echoed. A named request with any other op is malformed wire\n   222\t  input and is dropped.\n   223\t- The asking side gains one link operation, `resolve`: it sends the\n   224\t  request and answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`\n   225\t  until the answer is filed, exactly as `cursor` does (2.4). Its `ok`\n   226\t  carries the remote descriptor of the real stream over the same\n   227\t  channel. The reader then calls `attach!` on that descriptor. From\n   228\t  there everything is the unamended contract: the reflection's identity\n   229\t  is the ring's.\n   230\t- So the token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` is a channel descriptor and\n   231\t  a name, and the first act of following is one resolution.\n   232\t- Two peers serving one name serve two logical streams with two\n   233\t  identities. A restarted peer serves a new ring under a new identity:\n   234\t  the old reflection is answered `not-found`, marks itself gone, and the\n   235\t  reader resolves the name again. `dao.stream.md` is not amended.\n   236\t- **Nothing is put in the DHT.** The DHT is used as today, to fetch the\n   237\t  blobs of the index the trace names.\n   238\t\n   239\t**A relay may serve the trace, and needs no trust.** A trace is\n   240\tself-authenticating (section 5.2). Any node that follows a principal, has\n   241\ta socket and is publishing (`:publish?`) enters its own capacity-1 ring\n   242\tand maps the same name to it, holding the last trace it **installed**\n   243\t(5.5: confirmed and persisted), verbatim. It is that node's stream,\n   244\twritten by that node, with its own identity; the authorship of the value\n   245\tinside is the signature's. A fetch-only node serves nothing, as its banner\n   246\talready promises. No node is a relay by kind: a relay is a follower with a\n   247\tsocket.\n   248\t\n   249\t**Availability.**\n   250\t\n   251\t- Publisher online and reachable: a reader asks once per poll interval\n   252\t  (section 7). It observes a new head at the first poll whose request\n   253\t  and answer are both delivered. UDP promises neither; a lost datagram\n   254\t  costs the link's resend, or the next poll. No bound on observation is\n   255\t  claimed beyond that.\n   256\t- Publisher offline, a relay reachable: the reader observes the last head\n   257\t  that relay installed. It can load that head's index only as far as the\n   258\t  DHT still holds the blobs (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6).\n   259\t- Publisher offline, no relay: the reader observes nothing new. It keeps\n   260\t  the head it persisted and everything it linked. Nothing times out.\n   261\t- A reader that has never had contact and reaches no source has no head.\n   262\t  Section 5.6 says what a `require` does then.\n   263\t\n   264\tRejected: a signed mutable record under a DHT key (section 8, X1); both\n   265\tmedia at once (X2).\n   266\t\n   267\t### 5.2 Trace schema (decision 2)\n   268\t\n   269\tA trace is one plain value: an envelope and its proof, the shape a name\n   270\tassertion already has (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.1, 6.3).\n   271\t\n   272\t```clojure\n   273\t{:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   274\t                     :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n   275\t                     :yin.head/seq       1042}\n   276\t :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}}\n   277\t```\n   278\t\n   279\t- The key sets are closed. No other key is written, and a trace carrying\n   280\t  one is malformed.\n   281\t- No time is written: the publisher owns no clock. No address is written:\n   282\t  a reply goes to the observed source (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 6), and a\n   283\t  relay serves from its own.\n   284\t- The signed message is exactly\n   285\t  `UTF-8(\"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)`.\n   286\t  The prefix is domain separation: a name-envelope signature is never a\n   287\t  head proof, and the reverse.\n   288\t- Signing and verification are `yin.vm.linker.sign`'s Ed25519 primitive,\n   289\t  unchanged, with the prefix as the only new datum. The principal is its\n   290\t  own key, so verification needs nothing but the trace.\n   291\t- Verification refuses, as `:yin.head/bad-proof`: a signature that does\n   292\t  not verify, a malformed key or signature, and a principal that is not\n   293\t  `\"ed25519:\"` plus a well-formed key.\n   294\t\n   295\t**The sequence is derived, on both sides.**\n   296\t\n   297\t- `:yin.head/seq` is the greatest transaction `t` among the datoms of the\n   298\t  index the manifest names. It is a nonnegative integer, and **0 is a\n   299\t  valid sequence**: the transactor gives the first transaction `t = 0`\n   300\t  (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:117-131`), so a first published\n   301\t  index may have it. An index of no datoms has no sequence and no trace.\n   302\t- The publisher stores no counter and no trace. `deposit!` takes the\n   303\t  index's datoms, never a sequence, and computes it (`seq-of`).\n   304\t- A reader verifies it. After the index is loaded, a trace whose sequence\n   305\t  is not the greatest `t` of the loaded datoms is refused\n   306\t  `:yin.head/seq-mismatch` and is never installed. The sequence orders\n   307\t  traces before the load; the index confirms it after.\n   308\t- **What is proven and what is enforced.** That two HEAD writes of one\n   309\t  directory never share a greatest `t`, and that `t` survives a restart,\n   310\t  is established for the `yin.repl` index path only: publication follows\n   311\t  a committed transaction (`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:378-435`),\n   312\t  `rehydrate` preserves each `t` (`189-219`), and `(reset)` carries the\n   313\t  history over (`222-233`). H0 pins that by test. Nothing proves it of an\n   314\t  arbitrary plain-API composition that calls `:head-fn` itself.\n   315\t- So `deposit!` enforces it for every caller. Within one process it\n   316\t  refuses, signing nothing: a sequence equal to the last it signed with\n   317\t  another manifest (`:yin.head/seq-collision`), and a sequence below the\n   318\t  last it signed (`:yin.head/seq-regression`). The same manifest at the\n   319\t  same sequence is a no-op. A composition that moves HEAD without a new\n   320\t  transaction cannot publish that head; that is its obligation, stated\n   321\t  here, and the refusal is its notice.\n   322\t- **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts `t`, and with it the\n   323\t  sequence, exactly as `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5 says of the name\n   324\t  sequence. Readers refuse the new traces as stale. That section's\n   325\t  remedy is \"Hydrate the directory from the last published index\n   326\t  manifest before publishing, or use a new key\", and this design removes\n   327\t  hydration from the reader's path (D1). The remedy is kept as an\n   328\t  explicit publisher act, `--dht-recover` (5.8), and the sentence in 6.5\n   329\t  is amended to name it (H4).\n   330\t\n   331\tRejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter beside the key; a `:prev`\n   332\thash chain (section 8, X3).\n   333\t\n   334\t### 5.3 Retention (decision 3)\n   335\t\n   336\t**Capacity 1. The latest head wins and the ring itself coalesces.**\n   337\t\n   338\t- No reader needs a superseded head. Loading one is waste, and a head\n   339\t  history is the index's own history, already content-addressed.\n   340\t- **First contact.** The reader resolves the board name (5.1), attaches\n   341\t  the ring it names, and mints `:dao.stream/oldest`. On a capacity-1\n   342\t  ring the earliest retained position is the current head, so the first\n   343\t  `next` answers it. A ring nothing was deposited on answers `blocked`.\n   344\t- **A slow reader** holding a cursor across two deposits is answered\n   345\t  `gap` with the recovery cursor, which is the current head's position.\n   346\t  It adopts it. The gap is the honest statement \"heads were skipped\", and\n   347\t  it costs nothing: skipping them is the policy.\n   348\t- **A restarted source** has a new ring under a new identity. The old\n   349\t  identity is in no table, so the reader's reflection is answered\n   350\t  `not-found`, a present answer, and marks itself gone. The reader\n   351\t  resolves the name again, attaches the new ring and mints `:oldest`.\n   352\t  A name the peer does not serve yet is `not-found` at the resolution,\n   353\t  and is resolved again at the poll interval.\n   354\t- A reader keeps one reflection and one cursor per source and per\n   355\t  principal. A cursor never meets a ring it was not minted on, because\n   356\t  each ring has its own identity; were one to, the answer is\n   357\t  `cursor-mismatch` and the reader resolves again.\n   358\t\n   359\tRejected: a larger ring (nothing reads the extra elements); a\n   360\tcomplete-history log on a network-reachable entry (the wrong declared\n   361\tnature for a latest-wins value, `dao.stream.md`, Retention and Gaps).\n   362\t\n   363\t### 5.4 Publisher behaviour (decision 4)\n   364\t\n   365\t- **When.** One trace is deposited at every HEAD move: after the HEAD\n   366\t  write, in the same call, before any network result. This is the rule\n   367\t  HEAD itself follows (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6), and it is what the\n   368\t  owner asked for: the head, whatever it is. The deposit is a local\n   369\t  append; no round waits.\n   370\t- **Coalescing is the medium's.** A publisher does not batch. Ten rounds\n   371\t  between two polls leave one trace.\n   372\t- **At startup** a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits the trace\n   373\t  of that HEAD at once: manifest from HEAD, sequence from the recovered\n   374\t  datoms, signature from the key. A reader's first contact after the\n   375\t  publisher restarts therefore observes the head without any new round.\n   376\t- **Who deposits.** A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and\n   377\t  `:publish?`. Without a key it signs nothing, as it publishes no name.\n   378\t  Without `:publish?` it serves no board: a head whose blobs it will not\n   379\t  serve is a promise it cannot keep. Where the board may be served at\n   380\t  all is section 5.9's.\n   381\t- **Relation to `announce!` and the ledger ring: beside, not reuse, wrap\n   382\t  or replace.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue: every\n   383\t  put address and each window close, read once by `step`, for replication\n   384\t  accounting. It is unsigned because `dao.space.dht` holds no key and\n   385\t  knows nothing of `yin.*`, and both are right. Serving it would publish\n   386\t  every put address and still authenticate nothing. The head board is a\n   387\t  second ring with one job. The composition that calls `announce!` calls\n   388\t  the deposit beside it, in that order: `yin.repl.dht`'s `:head-fn`, and\n   389\t  the plain path `publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!`.\n   390\t- The publisher's own directory holds nothing new. HEAD stays the one\n   391\t  record of what its head is.\n   392\t\n   393\tRejected: deposit only on `:acknowledged` (a head would lag or never\n   394\tappear on a lossy mesh, and an acknowledgement is not retrievability);\n   395\tdeposit only on `yin.link/publish` (it is not the HEAD the owner named,\n   396\tand a reader's `q` over the followed index would lag the names it sees).\n   397\t\n   398\t### 5.5 Reader behaviour (decision 5)\n   399\t\n   400\t**Following.** A reader follows a principal by composition:\n   401\t`--dht-follow <64 hex>` or `:follow` to the plain API. Every followed\n   402\tprincipal must also be declared (`--dht-principal`, 7.1); a follow of an\n   403\tundeclared principal refuses startup. One trust root: the declaration.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Sources.** For each followed principal the reader resolves that\n   406\tprincipal's board name (5.1) through every configured peer\n   407\t(`--dht-peer`), or through the channel descriptors the plain API is\n   408\tgiven, and attaches the ring each answer names. A peer that does not\n   409\tserve the name answers `not-found`, and is asked again at the poll\n   410\tinterval: it may begin to.\n   411\t\n   412\t**Three things are kept apart per principal.** The first review found\n   413\tthem conflated; each has one job.\n   414\t\n   415\t<table>\n   416\t<tr><th>Thing</th><th>What it is</th><th>What it decides</th></tr>\n   417\t<tr><td>The <strong>floor</strong></td>\n   418\t<td>The sequence of the installed head, or <code>nil</code>: no head was\n   419\tever installed. It is read from the persisted trace (5.7).</td>\n   420\t<td>Replay and rollback, and nothing else.</td></tr>\n   421\t<tr><td>The <strong>candidates</strong></td>\n   422\t<td>At most <code>:max-candidates</code> verified traces above the\n   423\tfloor, process state.</td>\n   424\t<td>What to load next. A candidate proves nothing and raises\n   425\tnothing.</td></tr>\n   426\t<tr><td>The <strong>installed head</strong></td>\n   427\t<td>One trace: verified, loaded, its sequence confirmed by its index,\n   428\tand persisted.</td>\n   429\t<td>The snapshot set, the floor, and what a relay re-serves.</td></tr>\n   430\t</table>\n   431\t\n   432\t**The rule for one observed trace**, a pure function of the trace, the\n   433\tboard's principal, and the principal's floor:\n   434\t\n   435\t<table>\n   436\t<tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n   437\t<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not a\n   438\tnonnegative integer</td>\n   439\t<td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   440\t<tr><td>Principal is not the board's</td>\n   441\t<td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   442\t<tr><td>Signature does not verify</td>\n   443\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   444\t<tr><td>The floor is <code>nil</code></td>\n   445\t<td>a <strong>candidate</strong>, whatever its sequence, 0\n   446\tincluded</td></tr>\n   447\t<tr><td>Sequence below the floor</td>\n   448\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code>, dropped</td></tr>\n   449\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, the installed manifest</td>\n   450\t<td>a duplicate; nothing</td></tr>\n   451\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, another manifest</td>\n   452\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code> naming both; dropped</td></tr>\n   453\t<tr><td>Sequence above the floor</td>\n   454\t<td>a <strong>candidate</strong></td></tr>\n   455\t</table>\n   456\t\n   457\t- Traces from all sources go through the one rule, in any order.\n   458\t- **The floor is confirmed evidence only.** It moves when a head is\n   459\t  installed and at no other time. A candidate's sequence is a claim, and\n   460\t  a claim is never compared against a later trace.\n   461\t- **Rollback and replay.** An old, validly signed trace re-served by a\n   462\t  relay is `:stale` against the floor, and the floor is durable before\n   463\t  the head it belongs to is used (5.7). Section 9 states the one case\n   464\t  the floor cannot cover.\n   465\t\n   466\t**Candidates.**\n   467\t\n   468\t- A candidate equal to one already held, or to one in the rejected set\n   469\t  below, is dropped. Past `:max-candidates` the candidate of the least\n   470\t  sequence is dropped.\n   471\t- At most one candidate per principal loads at a time: the one of the\n   472\t  greatest sequence that is not waiting out a retry delay. Its load is\n   473\t  `dao.space.dht/load` with the covered-index walk, **held as a\n   474\t  candidate** (holders, below). A candidate load is not in the snapshot\n   475\t  set and no name resolves through it.\n   476\t- **Confirmed.** When the load is `:loaded` and the greatest `t` of its\n   477\t  datoms equals the trace's sequence, the candidate is confirmed. The\n   478\t  follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace and changes nothing else:\n   479\t  the head is not yet in the snapshot set, not on the relay ring, and\n   480\t  the floor has not moved. Section 5.7 says what makes it installed.\n   481\t- **Rejected.** When the load is `:loaded` and the sequence check fails,\n   482\t  the candidate is refused `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, discarded, and its\n   483\t  candidate hold released, so its index is forgotten and never\n   484\t  resolvable. Its envelope id joins the principal's **rejected set**\n   485\t  (at most `:max-rejected`, oldest dropped, process state), so the same\n   486\t  trace re-served at every poll is not loaded again. **Nothing else\n   487\t  changes: the floor is where it was**, and a valid trace of any\n   488\t  sequence above the floor is still a candidate. A signed trace claiming\n   489\t  sequence 1000000 over an index at 10 delays nothing but its own load.\n   490\t- **Unloadable.** A load that fails emits `:yin.head/unloadable` with\n   491\t  the failure as data. The candidate stays, and waits out a delay\n   492\t  counted in the node's ticks, doubling from `:repair-ticks` to\n   493\t  `:repair-max-ticks`, the bounds automatic repair already has. While it\n   494\t  waits, the next candidate down takes the load turn: an unloadable high\n   495\t  trace does not block a loadable lower one. No deadline ends the\n   496\t  retries; installation of a head at or above its sequence does.\n   497\t- **Superseded.** When a head is installed, every candidate at or below\n   498\t  its sequence is dropped and its hold released. One at the installed\n   499\t  sequence naming another manifest is reported `:equivocation` first.\n   500\t- Two candidates at one sequence naming two manifests are both\n   501\t  candidates. The first confirmed and installed wins; the other is then\n   502\t  the equivocation row. This one outcome depends on arrival order, and\n   503\t  the diagnostic names both manifests.\n   504\t\n   505\t**Holders: who needs a load (G5).** `dao.space.dht` records, per load,\n   506\tthe set of **holders** that need it. A holder is an opaque value to the\n   507\tnode.\n   508\t\n   509\t- `(dao.space.dht/load node address opts)` takes `:holder`, default\n   510\t  `:dao.space.dht/explicit`. Loading an address already loaded adds the\n   511\t  holder and starts nothing.\n   512\t- `(dao.space.dht/release node address holder)` removes one holder. The\n   513\t  record is dropped when its last holder is released; a record still\n   514\t  `:loading` is dropped when it ends. `forget` is `release` of\n   515\t  `:dao.space.dht/explicit`, so its existing callers see what they saw.\n   516\t- `(dao.space.dht/holders node address)` answers the set.\n   517\t- The follower holds a candidate as `[:yin.head/candidate principal]`\n   518\t  and an installed head as `[:yin.head/installed principal]`. It\n   519\t  releases only its own holders. Two principals naming one manifest, or\n   520\t  a head a user also loaded by hand, each keep the record alive through\n   521\t  their own holder.\n   522\t\n   523\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   524\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index whose load is\n   525\t`:loaded` **and held explicitly** (a load made by `load-index`, by hand\n   526\tor by `--dht-manifest`); and every index whose load is `:loaded` and\n   527\theld as the **installed** head of a followed principal. A load held only\n   528\tas a candidate is in no snapshot set. `yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots`\n   529\tchanges to read holders; H2 carries it. Resolution (7.3) is otherwise\n   530\tunchanged: one fold over that set under the declared principals.\n   531\t\n   532\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   533\t- Installation swaps the principal's installed holder from the previous\n   534\t  manifest to the new one in one step of the node. No fold sees both\n   535\t  heads of one principal, or neither.\n   536\t\n   537\t**A silent publisher.** The reader keeps the installed head, its index and\n   538\teverything linked. Nothing lapses and nothing is retracted. The follower\n   539\trecords, as data, the node tick reading at which each source last\n   540\tanswered; a drive that wants to judge liveness composes a `dao.lease`\n   541\tjudge over it (`dao.lease.md`). Nothing in the follower or the linker\n   542\treads that datum.\n   543\t\n   544\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   545\t\n   546\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   547\t\n   548\t- An installed head changes the snapshot set, and so the name\n   549\t  environment, at that step. A `require` that links resolves against the\n   550\t  set as it stands when it runs.\n   551\t- A require does not wait for a head that might exist: there is no bound\n   552\t  on that wait. The one wait is first contact, below.\n   553\t- **Consequence, stated plainly: a name may change meaning between two\n   554\t  requires.** A program that requires `alib` in two sessions, or in one\n   555\t  session before and after `(reset)`, may link two different modules.\n   556\t  The require's provenance carries the snapshot vector, so which index\n   557\t  bound the name is always data.\n   558\t- **Second consequence: dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When\n   559\t  `base` is republished and a reader follows both `base` and `app`,\n   560\t  `app`'s pinned address no longer matches and its require refuses\n   561\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4), until `app`\n   562\t  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n   563\t- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n   564\t  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n   565\t  principal, as a require under a DHT link source already may (R10). It\n   566\t  is re-checked when a head of a principal it waits on is installed\n   567\t  (R11's shape, a new event kind). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it\n   568\t  as they end any pending run.\n   569\t- **On demand.** `(yin.head/heads)` answers what is installed, what is\n   570\t  confirmed and awaiting its write, the candidates, and the last\n   571\t  refusal, per principal.\n   572\t\n   573\t**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is the escape hatch, redefined:\n   574\tthis run loads that index as a snapshot before evaluation is admitted,\n   575\tfollows no head, and writes no head record. It is one-shot and never\n   576\tsaved. A reproducible run names its index every time it starts. A direct\n   577\tentry (`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name and still wins\n   578\tover the fold.\n   579\t\n   580\tRejected: fetch the head on every require (a require would wait on a\n   581\tpublisher that owes no answer); refresh only on demand (it is not\n   582\t\"always\").\n   583\t\n   584\t### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n   585\t\n   586\t**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\n   587\ttrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n   588\t\n   589\t```clojure\n   590\t{:version 1\n   591\t :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n   592\t```\n   593\t\n   594\t- It is evidence, not a conclusion: the floor, the manifest and the\n   595\t  proof a relay re-serves are all read from it.\n   596\t- **Durable before used.** A confirmed head (5.5) is installed in this\n   597\t  order, and in no other:\n   598\t  1. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace.\n   599\t  2. The node's owner replaces `heads.edn` atomically, with that trace\n   600\t     in the principal's place.\n   601\t  3. Only when the write has returned does the owner call\n   602\t     `(head/install follower node principal trace)`. That call moves the\n   603\t     installed holder (the head enters the snapshot set), sets the\n   604\t     floor, appends the trace to the relay ring when the node serves\n   605\t     one, and emits `:installed`.\n   606\t- Nothing reads a confirmed head before step 3: no fold, no `require`,\n   607\t  no relay, no line that says it is installed.\n   608\t- **A crash before step 2** restarts at the previous head. The newer one\n   609\t  was never used, so nothing rolled back. **A crash after step 2**\n   610\t  restarts at the newer head, which is local and loads without a fetch.\n   611\t  No crash point restarts below a head that was ever exposed.\n   612\t- **A write that fails** installs nothing. The owner reports\n   613\t  `:yin.head/unpersisted` with the host's reason as data, the head stays\n   614\t  confirmed, the previous head stays installed and served, and the owner\n   615\t  tries the write again after the node's repair delay, doubling to its\n   616\t  bound. A newer confirmed head replaces the waiting one. No head is\n   617\t  ever installed on a promise to persist it later.\n   618\t- **A composition with no durable directory** (a test, a solo\n   619\t  experiment) says so: `(head/follow node {:heads :volatile ...})`. Its\n   620\t  owner calls `install` on `:confirmed` with no write. Its floor does\n   621\t  not survive a restart, by its own declaration, and it serves no relay\n   622\t  ring: a relay re-serves only what it holds durably.\n   623\t- At startup each record is verified again, its principal checked against\n   624\t  the followed set, and its index loaded from the local store, held as\n   625\t  installed. The blobs are local, so the load fetches nothing. A record\n   626\t  that fails verification refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back\n   627\t  to no floor.\n   628\t- A record for a principal no longer followed is dropped at the next\n   629\t  write.\n   630\t- An index loaded by hand, `(dao.space.dht/load-index m)`, is held\n   631\t  explicitly and stays process state. It is a pin made at the prompt,\n   632\t  and a restart forgets it. The follower never releases it.\n   633\t\n   634\tRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index.\n   635\tThe write would move the reader's HEAD; two nodes that follow each other\n   636\twould then move each other's heads without end.\n   637\t\n   638\t**Relink: a session keeps what it linked. `(reset)` is the way to the\n   639\tnew module.**\n   640\t\n   641\t- A linked module is never replaced under a live session. Its manifest\n   642\t  address is in the session's module registry, other modules are\n   643\t  discharged against that address (7.4), and code already running closed\n   644\t  over it.\n   645\t- `yin.vm` learns nothing. The repeat `(require 'alib)` of a linked name\n   646\t  stays what it is today, at the VM, with no consultation of any head.\n   647\t- **The change is reported, never silent.** When an installed head makes\n   648\t  a name linked in the session resolve to another address, the shell\n   649\t  prints one line: the name, the linked address, the resolved address,\n   650\t  the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then `(require ...)`\n   651\t  links it. The set is derived, the registry's `{name address}` against\n   652\t  the fold, and stored nowhere. `(yin.head/moved)` answers it on demand.\n   653\t\n   654\tRejected: relink in place (needs module unload and a rule for\n   655\tcontinuations captured against the old address; neither exists);\n   656\trefusing the repeat require (it would put head awareness in the VM's\n   657\trequire path, and fail a program for what a remote party did).\n   658\t\n   659\t### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n   660\t\n   661\t- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`. The publisher prints it\n   662\t  once, when its socket binds, not after each publication: it no longer\n   663\t  changes. A three-part token is refused with the message that a manifest\n   664\t  is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n   665\t- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n   666\t  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>`.\n   667\t- **Its first manifest** comes from the first trace it honours, by 5.5.\n   668\t  The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed principal,\n   669\t  whether a head is installed. It does not block the prompt on a\n   670\t  publisher: 5.6 parks the require instead.\n   671\t- **`--dht-manifest` with a saved `--dht-follow`**: the pin wins for that\n   672\t  run. The banner says following is suspended and `heads.edn` is neither\n   673\t  read as a snapshot nor written.\n   674\t- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, as now. It gains `--dht-follow`\n   675\t  (repeatable). It never holds a head; `heads.edn` does.\n   676\t  `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot, and `--dht-recover` is one-shot.\n   677\t- **Hydration into HEAD leaves the reader's path** (D1). No join, no\n   678\t  follow and no `--dht-manifest` writes HEAD. A pinned index that cannot\n   679\t  load still refuses the shell, as now.\n   680\t- **`--dht-recover <address>` is the publisher's recovery**, and the one\n   681\t  remaining writer of a HEAD the directory did not compute. It is what\n   682\t  hydration was, restricted to the case it was right for: a principal\n   683\t  restoring its own directory. It is one-shot and never saved; it needs\n   684\t  `--dht-key` and a peer; it refuses a directory whose HEAD already\n   685\t  names another index, as `refused-head` does today; it loads the index,\n   686\t  writes HEAD, rehydrates the indexer with every `t` preserved, and only\n   687\t  then admits evaluation. The node's startup deposit then signs that\n   688\t  manifest at its own greatest `t`, which is the trace readers already\n   689\t  hold, so they see a duplicate and then the next round. It does not\n   690\t  check that the index is the principal's latest: the operator names it,\n   691\t  as 6.5 always required. `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's remedy sentence\n   692\t  is amended to name this flag (H4).\n   693\t- **Where a board is served.** Until slice H5 lands, a node enters a\n   694\t  table only on a socket bound to a loopback address (5.9). On any other\n   695\t  bind the node serves no board, relays none, prints no token, and its\n   696\t  banner says so. Following is unaffected: a reader may ask from any\n   697\t  bind.\n   698\t- The banner of a publishing node with a key states that its head is\n   699\t  served to any peer that asks its address, and the banner of a following\n   700\t  node names whom it follows and that it re-serves their heads when it\n   701\t  publishes.\n   702\t\n   703\t### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n   704\t\n   705\tSection 9 is the table. The rules it rests on:\n   706\t\n   707\t- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a key the reader\n   708\t  declared. Who served it is never evidence.\n   709\t- A head is installed only after its whole index is local, its sequence\n   710\t  is confirmed by that index, and its trace is durable (5.7).\n   711\t- The floor is the installed head's sequence. It never moves down, it\n   712\t  moves only at installation, and it is durable before it is relied on.\n   713\t  An unconfirmed claim never moves it.\n   714\t- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n   715\t\n   716\t**Exposure is staged, as the DHT's was (C4).** `dao.jing.dht.md`\n   717\tkept its socket loopback-only while its cookie was a forgeable stand-in\n   718\t(section 8, \"exposure stays loopback-only\") and permitted more only\n   719\tafter the slice that hardened it. The mirror on that socket is in the\n   720\tsame position today: it answers any source, an answer may be larger\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
