{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '720,1445p'\"","aggregated_output":"   720\tsame position today: it answers any source, an answer may be larger\n   721\tthan its request, and bounding attachments bounds memory, not reflected\n   722\ttraffic.\n   723\t\n   724\t- **H1 to H4 serve tables on loopback only.** `dao.space.dht/join`\n   725\t  refuses a `:table` with a `:bind-host` that is not a loopback literal,\n   726\t  `:dao.space.dht/table-not-loopback`. Every slice's tests run on\n   727\t  loopback or the mesh seam, so nothing waits on the restriction.\n   728\t- **H5 lifts it with a return-path proof**, and following across\n   729\t  machines is not complete before H5. The rule H5 must meet is the\n   730\t  DHT's own, without exception: a datagram from a source that has not\n   731\t  proven its return path causes no work, allocates no channel state, and\n   732\t  draws no reply larger than itself.\n   733\t- **The mechanism H5 is to use**, subject to its own review because it\n   734\t  amends a frozen contract: the node already holds the DHT's root secret\n   735\t  and its `cookie-for`, a pure function of the epoch and the observed\n   736\t  address. The asker already receives that cookie in every DHT reply\n   737\t  from the peer it follows, since that peer is its `--dht-peer`. So the\n   738\t  asker presents the cookie as an open key on each remote request, and\n   739\t  the node checks it for the observed source, current or previous epoch,\n   740\t  below the mirror, before any projection or channel ring exists. An\n   741\t  unproven datagram is dropped in silence; the asker's recovery is the\n   742\t  DHT's, a padded `:ping` to that peer. No second secret, no second\n   743\t  cookie protocol. It needs `dao.jing.dht` to expose the check and the\n   744\t  held cookie as data, and that is the amendment.\n   745\t\n   746\tRejected for H1: shipping the public mirror with a measured\n   747\tamplification ratio (measuring is not bounding); a pad-to-answer-size\n   748\trule alone (it bounds the ratio at one and still allocates channel\n   749\tstate for a spoofed source, which evict-oldest then turns into the\n   750\teviction of real askers).\n   751\t\n   752\t### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n   753\t\n   754\t**Portable `.cljc`, identical on the JVM, Node and Dart:** the trace, its\n   755\tsigned bytes and the rule of 5.5 (`yin.vm.linker.head`); the board and\n   756\tthe follower's step; the node's mirror and reflections\n   757\t(`dao.stream.remote`, `dao.stream.udp`, both portable today); the\n   758\trendering of `heads.edn`.\n   759\t\n   760\t**Host seams, all existing:** Ed25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`); the\n   761\tdatagram bind (`dao.stream.datagram.*`); the atomic file replace\n   762\t(`dao.space.store.fs`). No new seam is needed.\n   763\t\n   764\t**A browser** has no UDP and no Ed25519 primitive here: it follows\n   765\tnothing, as it resolves no signed name (6.4). A WebSocket source is the\n   766\troute when signed names reach a browser build.\n   767\t\n   768\t**ClojureDart traps to design against:**\n   769\t\n   770\t- `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a closing bracket and\n   771\t  read back by test on Dart; its reader refuses `{:k 1 }`.\n   772\t- Host branches are written `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` with\n   773\t  `:cljd` first; `:clj` alone does not exclude the Dart build.\n   774\t- A protocol or reify method never takes `[_ _]`; a second ignored\n   775\t  parameter is named.\n   776\t- No test reaches a private var by `#'ns/name` across namespaces; a\n   777\t  helper a test needs is public.\n   778\t- The sequence is an index `t`. It stays within 2^53 so the same integer\n   779\t  is exact on Node, and the vectors include a sequence above 2^32.\n   780\t- A manifest address decoded from the wire is compared with `=`, never\n   781\t  by identity: ClojureScript keywords are not interned across reads.\n   782\t- The proof is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` of the envelope\n   783\t  as decoded from the channel codec (`dao.stream.cbor`), a different\n   784\t  encoder. H0 asserts on all three hosts that a trace sent through the\n   785\t  channel codec verifies.\n   786\t\n   787\t## 6. The plain Clojure API\n   788\t\n   789\tThe one path, for any Clojure program:\n   790\t\n   791\t```text\n   792\tjoin -> publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!       (publisher)\n   793\tjoin -> follow -> step ... -> names -> load-module -> link (reader)\n   794\t```\n   795\t\n   796\t<table>\n   797\t<tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   798\t<tr><td><code>(yin.vm.linker.head/trace key manifest seq)</code></td>\n   799\t<td>The signed trace of 5.2. Pure; <code>deposit!</code> is its one\n   800\tcaller outside tests.</td></tr>\n   801\t<tr><td><code>(head/verify trace)</code></td>\n   802\t<td>True or false; never throws.</td></tr>\n   803\t<tr><td><code>(head/seq-of datoms)</code></td>\n   804\t<td>The greatest <code>t</code>, the sequence of the index holding\n   805\tthem; <code>nil</code> for no datoms.</td></tr>\n   806\t<tr><td><code>(head/judge floor installed principal trace)</code></td>\n   807\t<td>The rule of 5.5: <code>:candidate</code>, <code>:duplicate</code> or\n   808\ta refusal, as data. <code>floor</code> is a sequence or\n   809\t<code>nil</code>.</td></tr>\n   810\t<tr><td><code>(head/board-name principal)</code></td>\n   811\t<td>The lookup name of 5.1.</td></tr>\n   812\t<tr><td><code>(head/deposit! node key manifest datoms)</code></td>\n   813\t<td>Compute the sequence, sign, and append to the node's own board;\n   814\trefuses <code>:yin.head/seq-collision</code>,\n   815\t<code>:yin.head/seq-regression</code>, <code>:yin.head/empty</code>,\n   816\t<code>:yin.head/no-key</code> (5.2).</td></tr>\n   817\t<tr><td><code>(head/follow node opts)</code></td>\n   818\t<td>Compose a follower over the node: <code>:follow</code> principals,\n   819\t<code>:sources</code> channel descriptors (default: the node's peers),\n   820\t<code>:heads</code> the persisted records or <code>:volatile</code>.\n   821\t</td></tr>\n   822\t<tr><td><code>(head/step follower node)</code></td>\n   823\t<td>One pass, called by the node's one owner after\n   824\t<code>dao.space.dht/step</code>: answers\n   825\t<code>[follower node events]</code>. It confirms; it never installs.\n   826\t</td></tr>\n   827\t<tr><td><code>(head/install follower node principal trace)</code></td>\n   828\t<td>Step 3 of 5.7, called by the owner after its write returned:\n   829\tanswers <code>[follower node events]</code>. Refused unless that trace\n   830\tis the principal's confirmed head.</td></tr>\n   831\t<tr><td><code>(head/heads follower)</code></td>\n   832\t<td>Per principal: <code>:installed</code>, <code>:confirmed</code>,\n   833\t<code>:candidates</code>, the last refusal, each source's last answer\n   834\treading.</td></tr>\n   835\t<tr><td><code>(head/records follower principal trace)</code></td>\n   836\t<td>The value <code>heads.edn</code> holds once that confirmed trace is\n   837\twritten: the installed traces with this one in its principal's\n   838\tplace.</td></tr>\n   839\t<tr><td><code>(head/moved names registry)</code></td>\n   840\t<td>The linked names whose resolved address differs (5.7).</td></tr>\n   841\t</table>\n   842\t\n   843\t`dao.space.dht` gains payload-agnostic things only and learns nothing\n   844\tabout heads: a `:table` and a `:names` option to `join` (a mirror table\n   845\tand a name map the node's step answers on its socket, bounded as\n   846\tsection 7 says, on loopback until H5); `(dao.space.dht/resolve node\n   847\tchannel name)` and `(dao.space.dht/reflect node descriptor)`, a\n   848\tresolution and a reflection through that same socket; and the holders\n   849\tof 5.5 (`:holder` on `load`, `release`, `holders`).\n   850\t\n   851\tEvents are plain maps under `:yin.head/event`: `:confirmed` (the trace;\n   852\tthe owner's cue to persist), `:installed` (principal, sequence,\n   853\tmanifest, the previous manifest), `:refused` (a reason of 5.5 or\n   854\t`:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, with the trace), and `:unloadable` (the load's\n   855\tfailure). `:yin.head/unpersisted` is the owner's own report, since the\n   856\twrite is the owner's.\n   857\t\n   858\t`yin.repl` adds a `yin.head` host module, `heads` and `moved`, each\n   859\targument checking and one call into the functions above\n   860\t(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10, the interface gate). The shell owns\n   861\tonly the file, the lines and the flags.\n   862\t\n   863\t## 7. Limits and defaults\n   864\t\n   865\t<table>\n   866\t<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   867\t<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed, not an option (5.3).</td>\n   868\t</tr>\n   869\t<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n   870\t<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> requests to one source for\n   871\tone principal. It bounds how often a reader asks, not how late it\n   872\tlearns: observation also needs both datagrams delivered (5.1).</td></tr>\n   873\t<tr><td><code>:max-candidates</code></td><td>4</td>\n   874\t<td>Candidates held per principal; the least sequence is dropped\n   875\t(5.5).</td></tr>\n   876\t<tr><td><code>:max-rejected</code></td><td>16</td>\n   877\t<td>Envelope ids of sequence-mismatched traces remembered per\n   878\tprincipal, oldest dropped; process state (5.5).</td></tr>\n   879\t<tr><td><code>:dao.stream.remote/resend-after</code></td><td>2</td>\n   880\t<td>The link's resend rule on UDP (<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code>\n   881\t2.5).</td></tr>\n   882\t<tr><td><code>:max-attachments</code></td><td>64</td>\n   883\t<td>Remote addresses the node's mirror holds channel state for;\n   884\tevict-oldest. An evicted asker's next request re-creates it. A memory\n   885\tbound only; it is not the exposure control (5.9).</td></tr>\n   886\t<tr><td>Head load retry</td>\n   887\t<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n   888\t<td>Doubling, as repair (5.5).</td></tr>\n   889\t<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n   890\t<td>A composition bound; more refuses startup.</td></tr>\n   891\t</table>\n   892\t\n   893\tFollowing never makes the node `busy?`. The owner's idle cadence must not\n   894\texceed `:head-poll-ticks`.\n   895\t\n   896\t## 8. What is rejected\n   897\t\n   898\t- **X1. The head under a key in the DHT** (a signed mutable record, as\n   899\t  BitTorrent's BEP 44). It survives the publisher going offline with no\n   900\t  relay configured, and that is its whole merit. Against it: it gives\n   901\t  `dao.jing.dht` roots, record validation, a sequence comparison and an\n   902\t  overwrite rule, each of which section 1 of that contract excludes and\n   903\t  each of which is semantics in the syntax layer; it is the\n   904\t  mutable-pointer service the owner's second sentence sets aside; and it\n   905\t  changes wire v1. A relay gives the same availability with no new\n   906\t  mechanism.\n   907\t- **X2. Both media.** Two paths to one fact need a rule for when they\n   908\t  disagree. The sequence already is that rule, so the second medium adds\n   909\t  cost and no property.\n   910\t- **X3. A hash chain (`:prev`).** It would let a reader audit a\n   911\t  publisher's history of heads. Nothing here needs that history, the\n   912\t  chain is not derivable from the index, and a capacity-1 ring could not\n   913\t  serve its links.\n   914\t- **X4. The signature in `m`.** C1.\n   915\t- **X5. Hydrating the followed head into the reader's HEAD.** D1.\n   916\t- **X6. A head operation on `dao.stream.apply` or an rpc.** It would put\n   917\t  a transport concept behind an apply word (owner, 2026-09-29) and make\n   918\t  the head a service. A ring served as itself needs neither.\n   919\t- **X7. A new DHT wire op (`:head`).** X1's objections, plus a version\n   920\t  break for a datum the socket's other interpreter already carries.\n   921\t- **X8. Push.** `dao.stream.remote.md` section 1 lists readiness\n   922\t  notification as a non-goal. The follower polls at a bounded cadence.\n   923\t- **X9. The board name as `:dao.stream/identity`** (this document's\n   924\t  first draft). C3: one string would name many logical streams.\n   925\t- **X10. The ring's identity in the token.** It needs no amendment and\n   926\t  is honest, but the identity is new at every restart, so the token\n   927\t  would be too, and the manifest would only have been traded for another\n   928\t  thing to hand over.\n   929\t- **X11. A floor that includes unconfirmed claims** (the first draft's\n   930\t  \"wanted\" sequence). A replayed, validly signed, wrong trace would then\n   931\t  shut out every true head below its claim.\n   932\t- **X12. Install, then persist** (the first draft). A reader that used\n   933\t  head 20 could restart at 19 and be held there by a withholding relay.\n   934\t\n   935\t## 9. Failure modes\n   936\t\n   937\t<table>\n   938\t<tr><th>Event</th><th>What the reader does</th><th>What it cannot do</th>\n   939\t</tr>\n   940\t<tr><td>A forged trace (no key)</td>\n   941\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>; dropped.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   942\t<tr><td>A trace for an undeclared principal</td>\n   943\t<td>Never asked for: only followed, declared principals have a\n   944\treflection. One arriving on a board is\n   945\t<code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code>.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   946\t<tr><td>A stale trace re-served (replay)</td>\n   947\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code> against the persisted floor.</td>\n   948\t<td><strong>First contact has no floor.</strong> A relay may hand a new\n   949\treader an old, valid head, and the reader installs it. It asks every\n   950\tsource and keeps the greatest sequence; the publisher's own address in\n   951\tthe token is the mitigation. No trace says how new it is.</td></tr>\n   952\t<tr><td>A publisher that rewinds (signs an older index)</td>\n   953\t<td>The older index has a lower <code>t</code>: <code>:stale</code>. A\n   954\thigher sequence on an old index is <code>:yin.head/seq-mismatch</code>\n   955\tafter the load.</td>\n   956\t<td>A key holder can build a new index with a higher <code>t</code> that\n   957\tomits anything. The reader trusts a declared principal for its own head;\n   958\tthat is what declaring means.</td></tr>\n   959\t<tr><td>A signed trace whose sequence is wrong, replayed by anyone</td>\n   960\t<td>A candidate, loaded, refused <code>:yin.head/seq-mismatch</code>,\n   961\tdiscarded, remembered as rejected. The floor does not move; true heads\n   962\tabove the floor install as they would have. Its index is never\n   963\tresolvable.</td>\n   964\t<td>Avoid loading it once per process: the rejected set is not\n   965\tpersisted.</td></tr>\n   966\t<tr><td>Two manifests at one sequence</td>\n   967\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code>; the installed head stays.</td>\n   968\t<td>It cannot tell which the publisher meant. A greater sequence ends\n   969\tit.</td></tr>\n   970\t<tr><td>A crash while a head is being installed</td>\n   971\t<td>Before the write: the previous head, and the new one was never\n   972\tused. After the write: the new head. Never below a head that was\n   973\texposed (5.7).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   974\t<tr><td><code>heads.edn</code> cannot be written</td>\n   975\t<td><code>:yin.head/unpersisted</code>; nothing is installed; the\n   976\tprevious head stays; the write is retried at the bounded delay.</td>\n   977\t<td>Advance while the directory refuses writes.</td></tr>\n   978\t<tr><td>Two principals, or a principal and a hand-made pin, name one\n   979\tmanifest</td>\n   980\t<td>Each holds the load; moving one releases only its own holder\n   981\t(5.5).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   982\t<tr><td>A relay that withholds</td>\n   983\t<td>Nothing arrives from it; other sources are unaffected.</td>\n   984\t<td><strong>Withholding is undetectable</strong> when every source\n   985\twithholds: silence and \"no new head\" look the same, and no timeout may\n   986\ttell them apart here.</td></tr>\n   987\t<tr><td>A trace naming a manifest nobody holds</td>\n   988\t<td><code>:yin.head/unloadable</code>; the previous head stays; retry\n   989\tat the bounded delay; a relay never re-serves it, having not installed\n   990\tit.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   991\t<tr><td>The publisher's directory is lost, the key kept</td>\n   992\t<td>New traces from a directory begun empty are <code>:stale</code>.\n   993\tAfter the publisher recovers with <code>--dht-recover</code> (5.8) its\n   994\ttraces continue from the old sequence.</td>\n   995\t<td>Recover on its own, short of removing the principal's record from\n   996\t<code>heads.edn</code> by hand. If the publisher cannot name its last\n   997\tmanifest, the remedy is a new key (6.5).</td></tr>\n   998\t<tr><td>The key is disclosed</td>\n   999\t<td>Whoever holds it moves the head. Remove the declaration\n  1000\t(6.5).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n  1001\t<tr><td>A source restarts</td>\n  1002\t<td><code>not-found</code> for the old ring's identity; the name is\n  1003\tresolved again and a fresh <code>:oldest</code> minted (5.3).</td>\n  1004\t<td>-</td></tr>\n  1005\t<tr><td>Spoofed askers on a serving socket</td>\n  1006\t<td>Until H5 there is no such socket off loopback: a table is refused\n  1007\ton any other bind (5.9). From H5 an unproven source draws no work and\n  1008\tno reply larger than its datagram.</td>\n  1009\t<td>Follow across machines before H5.</td></tr>\n  1010\t<tr><td>Anyone reads the board</td>\n  1011\t<td>By design. A principal's head is public where its node is reachable,\n  1012\tas its index already is under <code>--dht-publish</code>.</td>\n  1013\t<td>Restrict readers, until a gate is composed on the entry\n  1014\t(<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> section 7).</td></tr>\n  1015\t</table>\n  1016\t\n  1017\t## 10. Implementation slices\n  1018\t\n  1019\tOrder: **H0 -> H1 -> H2 -> H3 -> H4**, with **H5** after H1 at any\n  1020\tpoint (section 12, question 5). Each lands alone with the tree green on\n  1021\tthe JVM, Node and Dart. H0 and H1 own disjoint files. H1 to H4 serve on\n  1022\tloopback only; following across machines is complete at H5.\n  1023\t\n  1024\t**H0 -- the trace and its rule.** Pure, host neutral. Files: new\n  1025\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`trace`, `verify`, `seq-of`, `judge`,\n  1026\t`board-name`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (the head domain\n  1027\tprefix), `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`, new\n  1028\t`test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`. Complete when:\n  1029\t- **Sequence zero.** With a `nil` floor, a verified trace of sequence 0\n  1030\t  is a candidate. With floor 0, sequence 1 is a candidate, sequence 0\n  1031\t  with the installed manifest a duplicate, and sequence 0 with another\n  1032\t  manifest `:equivocation`. A negative or non-integer sequence is\n  1033\t  `:malformed`. `seq-of` of an index of one transaction is 0, asserted\n  1034\t  against `dao.space.transactor`'s first `t`, and `seq-of` of no datoms\n  1035\t  is `nil`.\n  1036\t- One trace under RFC 8032 TEST 1's seed is in the vectors with its\n  1037\t  canonical bytes, signed message and signature, asserted byte for byte\n  1038\t  on all three hosts; one has a sequence above 2^32.\n  1039\t- Tampering with the principal, manifest or sequence, one flipped\n  1040\t  signature bit, another key, an extra key in either map, and a\n  1041\t  name-envelope signature presented as a head proof each fail.\n  1042\t- A trace encoded and decoded by `dao.stream.cbor` verifies on each host.\n  1043\t- `judge` produces each row of the 5.5 table. It takes a floor and one\n  1044\t  trace and nothing else: no test can make its answer depend on a\n  1045\t  candidate.\n  1046\t- **The REPL path, proven.** `seq-of` over a rehydrated index equals\n  1047\t  `seq-of` before the restart, and a further round raises it. Two HEAD\n  1048\t  writes of one directory never share it, across rounds, name\n  1049\t  transactions, `(reset)` and the unwritten-row recovery of\n  1050\t  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1. **If any HEAD move is found that commits\n  1051\t  no transaction, this slice stops and reports it: the derived sequence\n  1052\t  does not hold and section 5.2 is reopened.**\n  1053\t- **The plain path, enforced.** `deposit!`'s refusals are specified\n  1054\t  here and implemented in H2: H0 fixes their codes and the rule that no\n  1055\t  public function signs a caller-supplied sequence onto a board.\n  1056\t\n  1057\t**H1 -- the node serves, names and reflects streams; loads have\n  1058\tholders.** Files: `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc` (the named\n  1059\t`descriptor` request, the link's `resolve`),\n  1060\t`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc` (`:table`, `:names`, `resolve`, `reflect`,\n  1061\tthe port and mirror in `step`; `:holder`, `release`, `holders`),\n  1062\t`test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc`, `test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc`,\n  1063\t`test/dao/jing/dht/mesh.cljc` (carry non-DHT datagrams). Nothing here\n  1064\tnames a head.\n  1065\t\n  1066\tContract amendments H1 carries, each in its governing document:\n  1067\t\n  1068\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` status line: implemented, not a design target.\n  1069\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` 2: a fifth composition concept, the **name\n  1070\t  map** `{name identity}`, beside the table.\n  1071\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.1: the named request shape of 5.1; \"Wire ops\n  1072\t  are exactly four\" stands, and a name rides `descriptor` only; the\n  1073\t  `not-found` error may carry `:dao.stream.remote/name` in place of\n  1074\t  `:dao.stream/identity`.\n  1075\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.3: a step 0, resolve a name through the name\n  1076\t  map to an entry or answer `not-found`; a named request with another op\n  1077\t  is malformed.\n  1078\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.4: the link's `resolve`, its retry answer and\n  1079\t  its `ok` carrying a remote descriptor over the link's own channel.\n  1080\t- `dao.stream.remote.md` 5: the head board as one more convention, and\n  1081\t  the sentence that a name is lookup data and never a\n  1082\t  `:dao.stream/identity`.\n  1083\t- `dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6: the node as a composition of both\n  1084\t  interpreters on one socket.\n  1085\t- `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10: `load`'s `:holder`, `release`,\n  1086\t  `holders`, and `forget` as the release of the explicit holder.\n  1087\t- `dao.stream.md`: none. If the reviewer of H1 finds one is needed, the\n  1088\t  slice stops: the design claims the stream contract is untouched.\n  1089\t\n  1090\tComplete when:\n  1091\t- Two mesh nodes: B resolves a name in A's name map, attaches the\n  1092\t  descriptor answered, and reads the capacity-1 ring's value; a second\n  1093\t  deposit is read; three deposits between reads give `gap` then the\n  1094\t  newest.\n  1095\t- **Identity.** The reflection's `descriptor` answers the ring's own\n  1096\t  identity, never the name. Two nodes serving one name answer two\n  1097\t  identities, and a cursor minted on one is `cursor-mismatch` on the\n  1098\t  other. No value on the wire carries the name under\n  1099\t  `:dao.stream/identity`.\n  1100\t- A restarted A: B's reflection is answered `not-found` and is gone; B\n  1101\t  resolves again, gets a new identity, and reads the new ring. An\n  1102\t  unmapped name is `not-found` with the name; a name mapped to an\n  1103\t  identity the table lacks is the same; a named `next` is dropped; a\n  1104\t  writer op on the `#{:reader}` entry is `no-surface`.\n  1105\t- **Loopback only.** `join` with a `:table` and a `:bind-host` that is\n  1106\t  not a loopback literal is refused `:dao.space.dht/table-not-loopback`,\n  1107\t  for IPv4 and IPv6 literals. `reflect` and `resolve` work from any\n  1108\t  bind.\n  1109\t- **Holders.** A load held by two holders survives the release of one\n  1110\t  and is dropped at the release of the other. A release during\n  1111\t  `:loading` drops the record when the load ends and emits its terminal\n  1112\t  event once. `forget` behaves as before for a load only it holds, and\n  1113\t  every existing load test passes unchanged.\n  1114\t- The DHT's existing mesh tests pass unchanged with the table in place,\n  1115\t  and neither interpreter produces a fact or an answer for the other's\n  1116\t  datagrams.\n  1117\t- A solo node opens no socket and serves no table.\n  1118\t- With more than `:max-attachments` asking addresses, the node's channel\n  1119\t  state never exceeds the bound and an evicted asker is answered again.\n  1120\t- Time advances only by appended ticks.\n  1121\t\n  1122\t**H2 -- deposit and follow, plain Clojure.** Files:\n  1123\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`deposit!`, `follow`, `step`,\n  1124\t`install`, `heads`, `records`, `moved`),\n  1125\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` (**`snapshots` changes**: HEAD, loads\n  1126\theld explicitly, loads held as installed; a candidate hold is excluded),\n  1127\tnew `test/yin/vm/linker/head_follow_test.cljc`,\n  1128\t`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md` (1, 7.2, 10, 13: D2). In the cases\n  1129\tbelow \"installs\" means the test's owner answered `:confirmed` by calling\n  1130\t`install`, as a `:volatile` composition does, unless the case says\n  1131\totherwise. Complete when:\n  1132\t- A publishes `alib`, deposits; B, given A's address and principal and\n  1133\t  no manifest, installs A's head and resolves `alib`. A republishes; B\n  1134\t  resolves the new address, with no call from the test but ticks and the\n  1135\t  owner's `install`. The mesh delivers every datagram in this case; a\n  1136\t  second case drops B's first request and its first answer and asserts\n  1137\t  the head still installs, later.\n  1138\t- **First transaction.** A publisher whose index holds one transaction\n  1139\t  deposits sequence 0; a first-contact reader installs it; the next\n  1140\t  round's sequence 1 replaces it.\n  1141\t- Ten deposits between two polls install one head, the last.\n  1142\t- **Candidates are not resolvable.** While B's candidate load of a new\n  1143\t  head is `:loaded` and not yet installed, `snapshots` does not list it\n  1144\t  and a name asserted only in it is `:absent`. The same holds between\n  1145\t  `:confirmed` and `install`.\n  1146\t- **A rejected candidate.** A signed trace of sequence 1000000 over an\n  1147\t  index whose greatest `t` is 10, holding a newer signed assertion of\n  1148\t  `alib`: it is loaded, refused `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, its load\n  1149\t  released and gone from the node's loads, `alib` never resolves through\n  1150\t  it, and the floor is unchanged. Re-served at the next poll, it starts\n  1151\t  no load.\n  1152\t- **Arrival orders, with that invalid trace X and true heads at 11 and\n  1153\t  12, from floor 10.** X then 11 then 12; 11 then X then 12; 11 then 12\n  1154\t  then X; X alone, then 11 after X is rejected; X while 11 is loading.\n  1155\t  Every order ends with 12 installed (11 in the fourth), floor equal to\n  1156\t  the installed sequence, X in the rejected set, and one `:installed`\n  1157\t  per head installed. The same orders run with a `nil` floor and with X\n  1158\t  unloadable instead of mismatched: the true heads install while X waits\n  1159\t  out its delay.\n  1160\t- **A superseded candidate.** With 11 loading, 12 arrives: 12 is\n  1161\t  installed; 11's candidate hold is released, its index is not in the\n  1162\t  snapshot set at any step, and a name asserted only in 11 never\n  1163\t  resolves.\n  1164\t- A relay R following A re-serves the installed trace; with A closed, a\n  1165\t  new reader C given only R installs it. R with `:publish?` false serves\n  1166\t  no board. R never serves a head that is only a candidate or only\n  1167\t  confirmed.\n  1168\t- R re-serving an older valid trace to B is `:stale`; to a first-contact\n  1169\t  reader also given A it loses to A's greater sequence; given R alone it\n  1170\t  is installed, and the test asserts that as the stated limit.\n  1171\t- A trace whose manifest no peer holds is `:unloadable`; the previous\n  1172\t  head stays; the load is started again only after the delay; a newer\n  1173\t  loadable head installs at once; R never serves the unloadable one.\n  1174\t- **Durable before used.** After `:confirmed` and before `install`: the\n  1175\t  floor, the snapshot set, `heads` under `:installed`, and R's relay ring\n  1176\t  are all unchanged. A follower dropped at that point and composed again\n  1177\t  from the old records is at the old head, and then installs the new\n  1178\t  one. A follower composed again from the new records, with `install`\n  1179\t  never called, is at the new head **with zero `:jing/get` on the\n  1180\t  request ring**, and refuses the older trace as `:stale`. `install` of\n  1181\t  a trace that is not the confirmed one is refused.\n  1182\t- **Shared loads.** Two followed principals whose heads name one\n  1183\t  manifest: one moves on, the other still resolves through the shared\n  1184\t  index. A head whose manifest the test also loaded with `load-index`:\n  1185\t  the principal moves on and the hand-made load is still in the\n  1186\t  snapshot set. A candidate naming a manifest that is another\n  1187\t  principal's installed head, rejected for this principal: the other's\n  1188\t  head is untouched.\n  1189\t- `deposit!` refuses `:yin.head/seq-collision`, `:seq-regression`,\n  1190\t  `:empty` and `:no-key`, each signing and appending nothing; the same\n  1191\t  manifest at the same sequence appends nothing.\n  1192\t- Two nodes following each other install each other's heads and reach\n  1193\t  quiescence: neither HEAD moves.\n  1194\t- The snapshot vector never holds two heads of one followed principal.\n  1195\t- `moved` names exactly the linked names whose address changed.\n  1196\t\n  1197\t**H3 -- the REPL.** Files: `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`,\n  1198\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/state.cljc`,\n  1199\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (the `yin.head` host module),\n  1200\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc` and `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` (the\n  1201\tfirst-contact pending), the tests beside each. Complete when:\n  1202\t- The token prints once at bind as `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`;\n  1203\t  `parse-token` refuses the three-part form with the pin message.\n  1204\t- `dht join <token>` saves `--dht-follow`; a bare restart follows again\n  1205\t  and resolves from `heads.edn` before any datagram is answered.\n  1206\t- `--dht-follow` of an undeclared principal refuses startup.\n  1207\t- A `(require 'alib)` typed before the first head parks, and completes\n  1208\t  on the head's install with no typed line; `(abandon)` ends it; an\n  1209\t  unrelated node event leaves `:checks` unchanged.\n  1210\t- An installed head that moves a linked name prints the one line of 5.7;\n  1211\t  the repeat require is unchanged; `(reset)` then `require` links the new\n  1212\t  address.\n  1213\t- `heads.edn` cases: absent, malformed, a record that fails verification,\n  1214\t  a record for an unfollowed principal. Each is read on Dart.\n  1215\t- **The write precedes the install.** The shell writes `heads.edn` on\n  1216\t  `:confirmed` and calls `install` only after the write returned; a test\n  1217\t  seam that fails the write asserts `:yin.head/unpersisted` is printed,\n  1218\t  nothing is installed, the previous head still resolves, and the write\n  1219\t  is tried again after the delay and then installs. A process killed\n  1220\t  between the write and the install restarts at the new head; one\n  1221\t  killed before the write restarts at the old head.\n  1222\t- On a bind that is not loopback the shell serves no board, prints no\n  1223\t  token, and its banner says why (5.8).\n  1224\t- The host module's functions hold no rule: a reviewer refuses any fold,\n  1225\t  verification or file write in `yin.repl.query`.\n  1226\t\n  1227\t**H4 -- the pin, the publisher's recovery, end to end.** Files:\n  1228\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`,\n  1229\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/state.cljc` (`--dht-recover` is one-shot),\n  1230\t`test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`,\n  1231\t`test/yin/vm/linker/dht_end_to_end_test.cljc`, `docs/design/` for\n  1232\t`yin.repl`, `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1 (the pointer here), and\n  1233\t`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5: the remedy of the \"key is kept and the\n  1234\tindex directory is lost\" row becomes \"Recover the directory with\n  1235\t`--dht-recover <manifest>` from the last published index manifest\n  1236\tbefore publishing, or use a new key.\" Complete when:\n  1237\t- `--dht-manifest` loads a snapshot held explicitly, follows nothing and\n  1238\t  leaves HEAD and `heads.edn` untouched; with a saved follow, the banner\n  1239\t  says following is suspended. No join and no follow writes HEAD.\n  1240\t- **Recovery.** A publisher with rounds to sequence `n` is stopped and\n  1241\t  its directory deleted. Started on an empty directory with its key and\n  1242\t  `--dht-recover <last manifest>`, it writes HEAD, deposits a trace a\n  1243\t  reader at floor `n` judges a duplicate, and its next round is sequence\n  1244\t  `n + 1`, which that reader installs. The same publisher started on an\n  1245\t  empty directory without the flag deposits a low sequence the reader\n  1246\t  refuses `:stale`. `--dht-recover` without a key, and into a directory\n  1247\t  whose HEAD names another index, each refuse startup. The flag is never\n  1248\t  saved.\n  1249\t- Process A publishes `alib`; process B joins with the two-part token\n  1250\t  and requires it on each of the four VMs, on the JVM and on Node. A\n  1251\t  republishes; B, after `(reset)`, links the new module. B restarted\n  1252\t  with A stopped still requires it.\n  1253\t- A process pinned with `--dht-manifest` links the first module after A\n  1254\t  republished.\n  1255\t- The in-process form passes on Dart over the mesh seam.\n  1256\t- Both processes bind loopback. The full three-host suite is run once,\n  1257\t  at landing.\n  1258\t\n  1259\t**H5 -- the return-path proof; tables leave loopback.** This slice\n  1260\tamends `dao.jing.dht.md`, a frozen contract, so it starts with its own\n  1261\tshort design note and review before any code; section 5.9 is its brief.\n  1262\tFiles, expected: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc` (expose the cookie check\n  1263\tfor an observed source and the cookie held for a peer, as data; a ping\n  1264\trequest), `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc` (the check below the mirror; the\n  1265\tasker's presentation; the loopback refusal removed),\n  1266\t`docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` sections 3 and 8,\n  1267\t`docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6. Complete when:\n  1268\t- A non-DHT datagram from a source with no valid cookie allocates no\n  1269\t  projection, no channel ring and no attachment, runs no mirror step,\n  1270\t  and draws no datagram at all. Asserted for a named resolution, a\n  1271\t  `descriptor`, a `cursor`, a `next` and a fragment.\n  1272\t- A source presenting a valid cookie for its observed address, under\n  1273\t  the current or the previous epoch, is answered. The same cookie from\n  1274\t  another address is not.\n  1275\t- A follower whose cookie expired stops being answered, pings through\n  1276\t  the DHT, and is answered again, with no call from the test but ticks.\n  1277\t- Flooding from more spoofed sources than `:max-attachments` evicts no\n  1278\t  real asker's channel state.\n  1279\t- The two-process test of H4 passes with A bound to a non-loopback\n  1280\t  address of the test host, on the JVM and on Node.\n  1281\t- `:dao.space.dht/table-not-loopback` no longer exists, and the banner\n  1282\t  and section 5.8 lose the restriction.\n  1283\t\n  1284\t## 11. Deferred\n  1285\t\n  1286\t- **Finding sources.** A reader asks the peers it was given. Rendezvous,\n  1287\t  and asking peers the DHT's routing table discovered, are\n  1288\t  `dao.stream.discovery.md`'s.\n  1289\t- **Return-path proof for a WebSocket or relay-pair source.** H5 covers\n  1290\t  the node's UDP socket only.\n  1291\t- **Recovering a publisher from its own trace.** A relay that installed\n  1292\t  the publisher's head holds a trace the publisher itself signed; a\n  1293\t  publisher that lost its directory and its last manifest address could\n  1294\t  follow its own principal to find it. Not built; `--dht-recover` takes\n  1295\t  the address from the operator.\n  1296\t- **A durable rejected set.** A wrong-sequence trace is loaded once per\n  1297\t  process (section 9).\n  1298\t- **Gating the board** by a ShiBi decision on the table entry.\n  1299\t- **Liveness.** A `dao.lease` composition over the follower's\n  1300\t  last-answer readings, for a drive that wants \"this publisher is gone\".\n  1301\t- **Relink in place**, with module unload and the fate of continuations\n  1302\t  captured against the old address.\n  1303\t- **Resolving a transitive require by its pinned address**\n  1304\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 13). Following makes its absence felt\n  1305\t  (5.6); it is the fix for the mismatch, and it is not designed here.\n  1306\t- **Collecting superseded indexes.** A forgotten head's blobs stay in the\n  1307\t  content log; `dao.jing.dht.md` defers garbage collection.\n  1308\t- **Key rotation and revocation** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 13). A\n  1309\t  rotation would move a head board's name.\n  1310\t- **A head history**, should an audit ever need one (X3).\n  1311\t- **Persisting loads made by hand.**\n  1312\t- **Push**, if `dao.stream.remote` ever gains readiness.\n  1313\t- **A browser follower** over a WebSocket source.\n  1314\t\n  1315\t## 12. Questions only the owner can answer\n  1316\t\n  1317\t1. **Does \"always\" reach into a live session?** This design keeps a\n  1318\t   session on the module it linked, reports the move, and links the new\n  1319\t   one after `(reset)` (5.7). If a repeat `(require 'alib)` must switch a\n  1320\t   running session to the new module, relink in place leaves section 11\n  1321\t   and needs its own design first.\n  1322\t2. **What should a bare `q` see after `dht join`?** One half is already\n  1323\t   ruled: the publisher's facts are a separate db-value, and combining\n  1324\t   them with the reader's own is an explicit union (`datom.world.md`,\n  1325\t   Tuples and Datoms). The open half is the default. Today a bare `q`\n  1326\t   answers the publisher's facts, because hydration made them the\n  1327\t   reader's own index. Here it answers the reader's own index only, and\n  1328\t   the publisher's is reached with `(dao.space.dht/q <manifest> ...)`,\n  1329\t   the manifest from `(yin.head/heads)`. Choose: (a) that; (b) a bare\n  1330\t   `q` in a following shell unions the reader's index with every\n  1331\t   installed head by default; (c) a named view, such as `(q :heads\n  1332\t   ...)`. Recommendation: (a) now, (c) when someone asks for it.\n  1333\t3. **May a publishing node re-serve the heads it follows by default?** It\n  1334\t   makes every publishing follower a relay, which is what keeps a head\n  1335\t   reachable while its publisher is offline. It also tells anyone who\n  1336\t   can ask that node whom it follows. Nothing ruled so far settles it.\n  1337\t   Recommendation: yes, with one flag to turn it off.\n  1338\t4. **Is every HEAD move the head you mean?** HEAD moves on every round\n  1339\t   that adds rows, so a follower loads a new index for each round the\n  1340\t   publisher evaluates, not only for each `yin.link/publish`. Your\n  1341\t   words, \"whatever the HEAD is\", read that way, and the design follows\n  1342\t   them; they were said before this cost was on the table. The\n  1343\t   alternative, a head only at each publish, is quieter, leaves a\n  1344\t   follower's view of the publisher's facts behind its names, and is no\n  1345\t   longer the HEAD. Recommendation: every HEAD move.\n  1346\t5. **Loopback first, or the return-path proof first?** As revised, a\n  1347\t   node serves its head on loopback only until slice H5 adds a\n  1348\t   return-path proof, because a public UDP mirror today answers\n  1349\t   spoofable sources with replies larger than their requests, which the\n  1350\t   DHT's own contract forbids. That is the DHT epic's staging, and it\n  1351\t   means **two machines cannot follow each other until H5**. H5 amends\n  1352\t   the frozen DHT contract and needs its own review. Choose the order:\n  1353\t   (a) H1 to H4 on loopback, then H5; (b) H5 directly after H1, so the\n  1354\t   first REPL slice already works across machines; (c) accept the\n  1355\t   unproven mirror on a public bind, as an explicit owner exception.\n  1356\t   Recommendation: (b) if cross-machine following is the first use you\n  1357\t   want, otherwise (a). Not (c).\n  1358\t6. **Is `--dht-recover` the recovery you want for a publisher?** It\n  1359\t   keeps today's remedy for a lost directory with a kept key, as an\n  1360\t   explicit publisher-only act, and is the one place a HEAD is written\n  1361\t   from a manifest handed in. The alternative is to drop it and make the\n  1362\t   remedy a new key, which every reader must then be told about.\n  1363\t   Recommendation: keep it.\n  1364\t\n  1365\t## Revision 1 (2026-10-05)\n  1366\t\n  1367\tThe independent review\n  1368\t(`collab/1791204360945-reviewer-architect-head-trace.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`)\n  1369\tblocked adoption on six P1 and two P2 findings. Each citation was\n  1370\tchecked against the tree; all eight hold and all eight are accepted. No\n  1371\tdecision the findings do not touch was changed.\n  1372\t\n  1373\t1. **Sequence zero (P1, accepted).** The first transaction is `t = 0`\n  1374\t   (`dao/space/transactor.cljc:117-131`). The floor is now a sequence or\n  1375\t   `nil`, and a `nil` floor accepts any valid sequence. Changed: 5.2,\n  1376\t   5.5, section 6, H0, H2.\n  1377\t2. **Candidates were resolvable (P1, accepted).** Every loaded index was\n  1378\t   in the snapshot set (`yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc:153-160`). Loads now\n  1379\t   have holders; the set is HEAD, explicit loads and installed heads.\n  1380\t   The first draft's \"no change to `snapshots`\" was wrong. Changed: G5,\n  1381\t   5.5, section 6, H1, H2.\n  1382\t3. **The board name as an identity (P1, accepted).** The first draft's\n  1383\t   C3 argued the mirror permitted it; the contract does not\n  1384\t   (`dao.stream.md`, Envelopes). A name is now resolved to the ring's\n  1385\t   real descriptor by a named `descriptor` request, an amendment to\n  1386\t   `dao.stream.remote.md` that H1 lists item by item. A restart is now\n  1387\t   `not-found` and a new resolution, not `cursor-mismatch`. Changed: C3,\n  1388\t   5.1, 5.3, section 6, X9, X10, section 9, H1.\n  1389\t4. **An unconfirmed claim poisoned the floor (P1, accepted).** The floor\n  1390\t   is the installed sequence only. Candidates are scheduling state,\n  1391\t   bounded, tried greatest first; a mismatch is discarded and\n  1392\t   remembered; an unloadable one does not block a lower loadable one.\n  1393\t   Changed: 5.5, 5.9, section 7, X11, section 9, H2.\n  1394\t5. **Rollback across a crash (P1, accepted).** Order is now confirm,\n  1395\t   persist, install; nothing reads a head before its trace is durable; a\n  1396\t   failed write installs nothing. Changed: 5.5, 5.7, section 6, X12,\n  1397\t   section 9, H2, H3.\n  1398\t6. **An ungated public reflector (P1, accepted).** Tables are served on\n  1399\t   loopback only through H4. A new slice H5 adds the return-path proof,\n  1400\t   reusing the DHT's cookie, and lifts the restriction. This changes\n  1401\t   what the first slices deliver, so it is question 5. Changed: C4, 5.8,\n  1402\t   5.9, section 7, section 9, H1, H3, H4, H5, section 11.\n  1403\t7. **Forget removed a shared load (P2, accepted).** Resolved with 2: the\n  1404\t   follower releases only its own holders. Changed: 5.5, 5.7, section 9,\n  1405\t   H1, H2.\n  1406\t8. **The recovery remedy was removed (P2, accepted).** Hydration leaves\n  1407\t   the reader's path only. `--dht-recover` keeps it as the publisher's\n  1408\t   explicit act, and the sentence in `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5 is\n  1409\t   amended in H4. Changed: section 3, 5.2, 5.8, section 9, H4,\n  1410\t   question 6.\n  1411\t\n  1412\tTwo claims were narrowed as the reviewer asked. The derived sequence is\n  1413\tproven for the `yin.repl` index path and **enforced** for every other\n  1414\tcaller by `deposit!`, which no longer takes a sequence (5.2, H0, H2).\n  1415\tObservation within a poll interval is conditional on delivery (5.1,\n  1416\tsection 7).\n  1417\t\n  1418\tFound while revising, not in the review:\n  1419\t\n  1420\t- **N1.** The first draft let a relay re-serve from a follower that had\n  1421\t  no durable directory. A relay now serves only what it holds durably\n  1422\t  (5.7).\n  1423\t- **N2.** With evict-oldest attachments and no return-path proof, a\n  1424\t  flood of spoofed sources evicts real askers. Bounding memory was\n  1425\t  presented as a mitigation; it is also a lever. H5 tests it (5.9).\n  1426\t- **N3.** The first draft printed the join token at bind on any socket.\n  1427\t  A node that serves no board must print no token (5.8).\n  1428\t- **N4.** `(reset)` was missing from the HEAD-move cases H0 must check\n  1429\t  for a shared greatest `t`.\n  1430\t\n  1431\t## Lineage\n  1432\t\n  1433\tThe tuple space made stigmergy precise; this makes one trace of it\n  1434\tdurable enough to follow. `announce!` was already the deposit, kept\n  1435\tprivate. A head board is the same act with an author and an audience: a\n  1436\tsigned value on a ring one agent writes and any agent may read, carried\n  1437\tby the mechanism that carries any stream. Datomic's connection hands a\n  1438\tpeer the newest database value; here no connection exists, only the\n  1439\ttrace, and each reader decides whose traces are worth following.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '466,900p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '466,900p'\"","aggregated_output":"   466\t**Candidates.**\n   467\t\n   468\t- A candidate equal to one already held, or to one in the rejected set\n   469\t  below, is dropped. Past `:max-candidates` the candidate of the least\n   470\t  sequence is dropped.\n   471\t- At most one candidate per principal loads at a time: the one of the\n   472\t  greatest sequence that is not waiting out a retry delay. Its load is\n   473\t  `dao.space.dht/load` with the covered-index walk, **held as a\n   474\t  candidate** (holders, below). A candidate load is not in the snapshot\n   475\t  set and no name resolves through it.\n   476\t- **Confirmed.** When the load is `:loaded` and the greatest `t` of its\n   477\t  datoms equals the trace's sequence, the candidate is confirmed. The\n   478\t  follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace and changes nothing else:\n   479\t  the head is not yet in the snapshot set, not on the relay ring, and\n   480\t  the floor has not moved. Section 5.7 says what makes it installed.\n   481\t- **Rejected.** When the load is `:loaded` and the sequence check fails,\n   482\t  the candidate is refused `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, discarded, and its\n   483\t  candidate hold released, so its index is forgotten and never\n   484\t  resolvable. Its envelope id joins the principal's **rejected set**\n   485\t  (at most `:max-rejected`, oldest dropped, process state), so the same\n   486\t  trace re-served at every poll is not loaded again. **Nothing else\n   487\t  changes: the floor is where it was**, and a valid trace of any\n   488\t  sequence above the floor is still a candidate. A signed trace claiming\n   489\t  sequence 1000000 over an index at 10 delays nothing but its own load.\n   490\t- **Unloadable.** A load that fails emits `:yin.head/unloadable` with\n   491\t  the failure as data. The candidate stays, and waits out a delay\n   492\t  counted in the node's ticks, doubling from `:repair-ticks` to\n   493\t  `:repair-max-ticks`, the bounds automatic repair already has. While it\n   494\t  waits, the next candidate down takes the load turn: an unloadable high\n   495\t  trace does not block a loadable lower one. No deadline ends the\n   496\t  retries; installation of a head at or above its sequence does.\n   497\t- **Superseded.** When a head is installed, every candidate at or below\n   498\t  its sequence is dropped and its hold released. One at the installed\n   499\t  sequence naming another manifest is reported `:equivocation` first.\n   500\t- Two candidates at one sequence naming two manifests are both\n   501\t  candidates. The first confirmed and installed wins; the other is then\n   502\t  the equivocation row. This one outcome depends on arrival order, and\n   503\t  the diagnostic names both manifests.\n   504\t\n   505\t**Holders: who needs a load (G5).** `dao.space.dht` records, per load,\n   506\tthe set of **holders** that need it. A holder is an opaque value to the\n   507\tnode.\n   508\t\n   509\t- `(dao.space.dht/load node address opts)` takes `:holder`, default\n   510\t  `:dao.space.dht/explicit`. Loading an address already loaded adds the\n   511\t  holder and starts nothing.\n   512\t- `(dao.space.dht/release node address holder)` removes one holder. The\n   513\t  record is dropped when its last holder is released; a record still\n   514\t  `:loading` is dropped when it ends. `forget` is `release` of\n   515\t  `:dao.space.dht/explicit`, so its existing callers see what they saw.\n   516\t- `(dao.space.dht/holders node address)` answers the set.\n   517\t- The follower holds a candidate as `[:yin.head/candidate principal]`\n   518\t  and an installed head as `[:yin.head/installed principal]`. It\n   519\t  releases only its own holders. Two principals naming one manifest, or\n   520\t  a head a user also loaded by hand, each keep the record alive through\n   521\t  their own holder.\n   522\t\n   523\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   524\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index whose load is\n   525\t`:loaded` **and held explicitly** (a load made by `load-index`, by hand\n   526\tor by `--dht-manifest`); and every index whose load is `:loaded` and\n   527\theld as the **installed** head of a followed principal. A load held only\n   528\tas a candidate is in no snapshot set. `yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots`\n   529\tchanges to read holders; H2 carries it. Resolution (7.3) is otherwise\n   530\tunchanged: one fold over that set under the declared principals.\n   531\t\n   532\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   533\t- Installation swaps the principal's installed holder from the previous\n   534\t  manifest to the new one in one step of the node. No fold sees both\n   535\t  heads of one principal, or neither.\n   536\t\n   537\t**A silent publisher.** The reader keeps the installed head, its index and\n   538\teverything linked. Nothing lapses and nothing is retracted. The follower\n   539\trecords, as data, the node tick reading at which each source last\n   540\tanswered; a drive that wants to judge liveness composes a `dao.lease`\n   541\tjudge over it (`dao.lease.md`). Nothing in the follower or the linker\n   542\treads that datum.\n   543\t\n   544\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   545\t\n   546\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   547\t\n   548\t- An installed head changes the snapshot set, and so the name\n   549\t  environment, at that step. A `require` that links resolves against the\n   550\t  set as it stands when it runs.\n   551\t- A require does not wait for a head that might exist: there is no bound\n   552\t  on that wait. The one wait is first contact, below.\n   553\t- **Consequence, stated plainly: a name may change meaning between two\n   554\t  requires.** A program that requires `alib` in two sessions, or in one\n   555\t  session before and after `(reset)`, may link two different modules.\n   556\t  The require's provenance carries the snapshot vector, so which index\n   557\t  bound the name is always data.\n   558\t- **Second consequence: dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When\n   559\t  `base` is republished and a reader follows both `base` and `app`,\n   560\t  `app`'s pinned address no longer matches and its require refuses\n   561\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4), until `app`\n   562\t  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n   563\t- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n   564\t  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n   565\t  principal, as a require under a DHT link source already may (R10). It\n   566\t  is re-checked when a head of a principal it waits on is installed\n   567\t  (R11's shape, a new event kind). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it\n   568\t  as they end any pending run.\n   569\t- **On demand.** `(yin.head/heads)` answers what is installed, what is\n   570\t  confirmed and awaiting its write, the candidates, and the last\n   571\t  refusal, per principal.\n   572\t\n   573\t**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is the escape hatch, redefined:\n   574\tthis run loads that index as a snapshot before evaluation is admitted,\n   575\tfollows no head, and writes no head record. It is one-shot and never\n   576\tsaved. A reproducible run names its index every time it starts. A direct\n   577\tentry (`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name and still wins\n   578\tover the fold.\n   579\t\n   580\tRejected: fetch the head on every require (a require would wait on a\n   581\tpublisher that owes no answer); refresh only on demand (it is not\n   582\t\"always\").\n   583\t\n   584\t### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n   585\t\n   586\t**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\n   587\ttrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n   588\t\n   589\t```clojure\n   590\t{:version 1\n   591\t :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n   592\t```\n   593\t\n   594\t- It is evidence, not a conclusion: the floor, the manifest and the\n   595\t  proof a relay re-serves are all read from it.\n   596\t- **Durable before used.** A confirmed head (5.5) is installed in this\n   597\t  order, and in no other:\n   598\t  1. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace.\n   599\t  2. The node's owner replaces `heads.edn` atomically, with that trace\n   600\t     in the principal's place.\n   601\t  3. Only when the write has returned does the owner call\n   602\t     `(head/install follower node principal trace)`. That call moves the\n   603\t     installed holder (the head enters the snapshot set), sets the\n   604\t     floor, appends the trace to the relay ring when the node serves\n   605\t     one, and emits `:installed`.\n   606\t- Nothing reads a confirmed head before step 3: no fold, no `require`,\n   607\t  no relay, no line that says it is installed.\n   608\t- **A crash before step 2** restarts at the previous head. The newer one\n   609\t  was never used, so nothing rolled back. **A crash after step 2**\n   610\t  restarts at the newer head, which is local and loads without a fetch.\n   611\t  No crash point restarts below a head that was ever exposed.\n   612\t- **A write that fails** installs nothing. The owner reports\n   613\t  `:yin.head/unpersisted` with the host's reason as data, the head stays\n   614\t  confirmed, the previous head stays installed and served, and the owner\n   615\t  tries the write again after the node's repair delay, doubling to its\n   616\t  bound. A newer confirmed head replaces the waiting one. No head is\n   617\t  ever installed on a promise to persist it later.\n   618\t- **A composition with no durable directory** (a test, a solo\n   619\t  experiment) says so: `(head/follow node {:heads :volatile ...})`. Its\n   620\t  owner calls `install` on `:confirmed` with no write. Its floor does\n   621\t  not survive a restart, by its own declaration, and it serves no relay\n   622\t  ring: a relay re-serves only what it holds durably.\n   623\t- At startup each record is verified again, its principal checked against\n   624\t  the followed set, and its index loaded from the local store, held as\n   625\t  installed. The blobs are local, so the load fetches nothing. A record\n   626\t  that fails verification refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back\n   627\t  to no floor.\n   628\t- A record for a principal no longer followed is dropped at the next\n   629\t  write.\n   630\t- An index loaded by hand, `(dao.space.dht/load-index m)`, is held\n   631\t  explicitly and stays process state. It is a pin made at the prompt,\n   632\t  and a restart forgets it. The follower never releases it.\n   633\t\n   634\tRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index.\n   635\tThe write would move the reader's HEAD; two nodes that follow each other\n   636\twould then move each other's heads without end.\n   637\t\n   638\t**Relink: a session keeps what it linked. `(reset)` is the way to the\n   639\tnew module.**\n   640\t\n   641\t- A linked module is never replaced under a live session. Its manifest\n   642\t  address is in the session's module registry, other modules are\n   643\t  discharged against that address (7.4), and code already running closed\n   644\t  over it.\n   645\t- `yin.vm` learns nothing. The repeat `(require 'alib)` of a linked name\n   646\t  stays what it is today, at the VM, with no consultation of any head.\n   647\t- **The change is reported, never silent.** When an installed head makes\n   648\t  a name linked in the session resolve to another address, the shell\n   649\t  prints one line: the name, the linked address, the resolved address,\n   650\t  the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then `(require ...)`\n   651\t  links it. The set is derived, the registry's `{name address}` against\n   652\t  the fold, and stored nowhere. `(yin.head/moved)` answers it on demand.\n   653\t\n   654\tRejected: relink in place (needs module unload and a rule for\n   655\tcontinuations captured against the old address; neither exists);\n   656\trefusing the repeat require (it would put head awareness in the VM's\n   657\trequire path, and fail a program for what a remote party did).\n   658\t\n   659\t### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n   660\t\n   661\t- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`. The publisher prints it\n   662\t  once, when its socket binds, not after each publication: it no longer\n   663\t  changes. A three-part token is refused with the message that a manifest\n   664\t  is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n   665\t- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n   666\t  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>`.\n   667\t- **Its first manifest** comes from the first trace it honours, by 5.5.\n   668\t  The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed principal,\n   669\t  whether a head is installed. It does not block the prompt on a\n   670\t  publisher: 5.6 parks the require instead.\n   671\t- **`--dht-manifest` with a saved `--dht-follow`**: the pin wins for that\n   672\t  run. The banner says following is suspended and `heads.edn` is neither\n   673\t  read as a snapshot nor written.\n   674\t- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, as now. It gains `--dht-follow`\n   675\t  (repeatable). It never holds a head; `heads.edn` does.\n   676\t  `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot, and `--dht-recover` is one-shot.\n   677\t- **Hydration into HEAD leaves the reader's path** (D1). No join, no\n   678\t  follow and no `--dht-manifest` writes HEAD. A pinned index that cannot\n   679\t  load still refuses the shell, as now.\n   680\t- **`--dht-recover <address>` is the publisher's recovery**, and the one\n   681\t  remaining writer of a HEAD the directory did not compute. It is what\n   682\t  hydration was, restricted to the case it was right for: a principal\n   683\t  restoring its own directory. It is one-shot and never saved; it needs\n   684\t  `--dht-key` and a peer; it refuses a directory whose HEAD already\n   685\t  names another index, as `refused-head` does today; it loads the index,\n   686\t  writes HEAD, rehydrates the indexer with every `t` preserved, and only\n   687\t  then admits evaluation. The node's startup deposit then signs that\n   688\t  manifest at its own greatest `t`, which is the trace readers already\n   689\t  hold, so they see a duplicate and then the next round. It does not\n   690\t  check that the index is the principal's latest: the operator names it,\n   691\t  as 6.5 always required. `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's remedy sentence\n   692\t  is amended to name this flag (H4).\n   693\t- **Where a board is served.** Until slice H5 lands, a node enters a\n   694\t  table only on a socket bound to a loopback address (5.9). On any other\n   695\t  bind the node serves no board, relays none, prints no token, and its\n   696\t  banner says so. Following is unaffected: a reader may ask from any\n   697\t  bind.\n   698\t- The banner of a publishing node with a key states that its head is\n   699\t  served to any peer that asks its address, and the banner of a following\n   700\t  node names whom it follows and that it re-serves their heads when it\n   701\t  publishes.\n   702\t\n   703\t### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n   704\t\n   705\tSection 9 is the table. The rules it rests on:\n   706\t\n   707\t- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a key the reader\n   708\t  declared. Who served it is never evidence.\n   709\t- A head is installed only after its whole index is local, its sequence\n   710\t  is confirmed by that index, and its trace is durable (5.7).\n   711\t- The floor is the installed head's sequence. It never moves down, it\n   712\t  moves only at installation, and it is durable before it is relied on.\n   713\t  An unconfirmed claim never moves it.\n   714\t- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n   715\t\n   716\t**Exposure is staged, as the DHT's was (C4).** `dao.jing.dht.md`\n   717\tkept its socket loopback-only while its cookie was a forgeable stand-in\n   718\t(section 8, \"exposure stays loopback-only\") and permitted more only\n   719\tafter the slice that hardened it. The mirror on that socket is in the\n   720\tsame position today: it answers any source, an answer may be larger\n   721\tthan its request, and bounding attachments bounds memory, not reflected\n   722\ttraffic.\n   723\t\n   724\t- **H1 to H4 serve tables on loopback only.** `dao.space.dht/join`\n   725\t  refuses a `:table` with a `:bind-host` that is not a loopback literal,\n   726\t  `:dao.space.dht/table-not-loopback`. Every slice's tests run on\n   727\t  loopback or the mesh seam, so nothing waits on the restriction.\n   728\t- **H5 lifts it with a return-path proof**, and following across\n   729\t  machines is not complete before H5. The rule H5 must meet is the\n   730\t  DHT's own, without exception: a datagram from a source that has not\n   731\t  proven its return path causes no work, allocates no channel state, and\n   732\t  draws no reply larger than itself.\n   733\t- **The mechanism H5 is to use**, subject to its own review because it\n   734\t  amends a frozen contract: the node already holds the DHT's root secret\n   735\t  and its `cookie-for`, a pure function of the epoch and the observed\n   736\t  address. The asker already receives that cookie in every DHT reply\n   737\t  from the peer it follows, since that peer is its `--dht-peer`. So the\n   738\t  asker presents the cookie as an open key on each remote request, and\n   739\t  the node checks it for the observed source, current or previous epoch,\n   740\t  below the mirror, before any projection or channel ring exists. An\n   741\t  unproven datagram is dropped in silence; the asker's recovery is the\n   742\t  DHT's, a padded `:ping` to that peer. No second secret, no second\n   743\t  cookie protocol. It needs `dao.jing.dht` to expose the check and the\n   744\t  held cookie as data, and that is the amendment.\n   745\t\n   746\tRejected for H1: shipping the public mirror with a measured\n   747\tamplification ratio (measuring is not bounding); a pad-to-answer-size\n   748\trule alone (it bounds the ratio at one and still allocates channel\n   749\tstate for a spoofed source, which evict-oldest then turns into the\n   750\teviction of real askers).\n   751\t\n   752\t### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n   753\t\n   754\t**Portable `.cljc`, identical on the JVM, Node and Dart:** the trace, its\n   755\tsigned bytes and the rule of 5.5 (`yin.vm.linker.head`); the board and\n   756\tthe follower's step; the node's mirror and reflections\n   757\t(`dao.stream.remote`, `dao.stream.udp`, both portable today); the\n   758\trendering of `heads.edn`.\n   759\t\n   760\t**Host seams, all existing:** Ed25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`); the\n   761\tdatagram bind (`dao.stream.datagram.*`); the atomic file replace\n   762\t(`dao.space.store.fs`). No new seam is needed.\n   763\t\n   764\t**A browser** has no UDP and no Ed25519 primitive here: it follows\n   765\tnothing, as it resolves no signed name (6.4). A WebSocket source is the\n   766\troute when signed names reach a browser build.\n   767\t\n   768\t**ClojureDart traps to design against:**\n   769\t\n   770\t- `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a closing bracket and\n   771\t  read back by test on Dart; its reader refuses `{:k 1 }`.\n   772\t- Host branches are written `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` with\n   773\t  `:cljd` first; `:clj` alone does not exclude the Dart build.\n   774\t- A protocol or reify method never takes `[_ _]`; a second ignored\n   775\t  parameter is named.\n   776\t- No test reaches a private var by `#'ns/name` across namespaces; a\n   777\t  helper a test needs is public.\n   778\t- The sequence is an index `t`. It stays within 2^53 so the same integer\n   779\t  is exact on Node, and the vectors include a sequence above 2^32.\n   780\t- A manifest address decoded from the wire is compared with `=`, never\n   781\t  by identity: ClojureScript keywords are not interned across reads.\n   782\t- The proof is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` of the envelope\n   783\t  as decoded from the channel codec (`dao.stream.cbor`), a different\n   784\t  encoder. H0 asserts on all three hosts that a trace sent through the\n   785\t  channel codec verifies.\n   786\t\n   787\t## 6. The plain Clojure API\n   788\t\n   789\tThe one path, for any Clojure program:\n   790\t\n   791\t```text\n   792\tjoin -> publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!       (publisher)\n   793\tjoin -> follow -> step ... -> names -> load-module -> link (reader)\n   794\t```\n   795\t\n   796\t<table>\n   797\t<tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   798\t<tr><td><code>(yin.vm.linker.head/trace key manifest seq)</code></td>\n   799\t<td>The signed trace of 5.2. Pure; <code>deposit!</code> is its one\n   800\tcaller outside tests.</td></tr>\n   801\t<tr><td><code>(head/verify trace)</code></td>\n   802\t<td>True or false; never throws.</td></tr>\n   803\t<tr><td><code>(head/seq-of datoms)</code></td>\n   804\t<td>The greatest <code>t</code>, the sequence of the index holding\n   805\tthem; <code>nil</code> for no datoms.</td></tr>\n   806\t<tr><td><code>(head/judge floor installed principal trace)</code></td>\n   807\t<td>The rule of 5.5: <code>:candidate</code>, <code>:duplicate</code> or\n   808\ta refusal, as data. <code>floor</code> is a sequence or\n   809\t<code>nil</code>.</td></tr>\n   810\t<tr><td><code>(head/board-name principal)</code></td>\n   811\t<td>The lookup name of 5.1.</td></tr>\n   812\t<tr><td><code>(head/deposit! node key manifest datoms)</code></td>\n   813\t<td>Compute the sequence, sign, and append to the node's own board;\n   814\trefuses <code>:yin.head/seq-collision</code>,\n   815\t<code>:yin.head/seq-regression</code>, <code>:yin.head/empty</code>,\n   816\t<code>:yin.head/no-key</code> (5.2).</td></tr>\n   817\t<tr><td><code>(head/follow node opts)</code></td>\n   818\t<td>Compose a follower over the node: <code>:follow</code> principals,\n   819\t<code>:sources</code> channel descriptors (default: the node's peers),\n   820\t<code>:heads</code> the persisted records or <code>:volatile</code>.\n   821\t</td></tr>\n   822\t<tr><td><code>(head/step follower node)</code></td>\n   823\t<td>One pass, called by the node's one owner after\n   824\t<code>dao.space.dht/step</code>: answers\n   825\t<code>[follower node events]</code>. It confirms; it never installs.\n   826\t</td></tr>\n   827\t<tr><td><code>(head/install follower node principal trace)</code></td>\n   828\t<td>Step 3 of 5.7, called by the owner after its write returned:\n   829\tanswers <code>[follower node events]</code>. Refused unless that trace\n   830\tis the principal's confirmed head.</td></tr>\n   831\t<tr><td><code>(head/heads follower)</code></td>\n   832\t<td>Per principal: <code>:installed</code>, <code>:confirmed</code>,\n   833\t<code>:candidates</code>, the last refusal, each source's last answer\n   834\treading.</td></tr>\n   835\t<tr><td><code>(head/records follower principal trace)</code></td>\n   836\t<td>The value <code>heads.edn</code> holds once that confirmed trace is\n   837\twritten: the installed traces with this one in its principal's\n   838\tplace.</td></tr>\n   839\t<tr><td><code>(head/moved names registry)</code></td>\n   840\t<td>The linked names whose resolved address differs (5.7).</td></tr>\n   841\t</table>\n   842\t\n   843\t`dao.space.dht` gains payload-agnostic things only and learns nothing\n   844\tabout heads: a `:table` and a `:names` option to `join` (a mirror table\n   845\tand a name map the node's step answers on its socket, bounded as\n   846\tsection 7 says, on loopback until H5); `(dao.space.dht/resolve node\n   847\tchannel name)` and `(dao.space.dht/reflect node descriptor)`, a\n   848\tresolution and a reflection through that same socket; and the holders\n   849\tof 5.5 (`:holder` on `load`, `release`, `holders`).\n   850\t\n   851\tEvents are plain maps under `:yin.head/event`: `:confirmed` (the trace;\n   852\tthe owner's cue to persist), `:installed` (principal, sequence,\n   853\tmanifest, the previous manifest), `:refused` (a reason of 5.5 or\n   854\t`:yin.head/seq-mismatch`, with the trace), and `:unloadable` (the load's\n   855\tfailure). `:yin.head/unpersisted` is the owner's own report, since the\n   856\twrite is the owner's.\n   857\t\n   858\t`yin.repl` adds a `yin.head` host module, `heads` and `moved`, each\n   859\targument checking and one call into the functions above\n   860\t(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10, the interface gate). The shell owns\n   861\tonly the file, the lines and the flags.\n   862\t\n   863\t## 7. Limits and defaults\n   864\t\n   865\t<table>\n   866\t<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   867\t<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed, not an option (5.3).</td>\n   868\t</tr>\n   869\t<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n   870\t<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> requests to one source for\n   871\tone principal. It bounds how often a reader asks, not how late it\n   872\tlearns: observation also needs both datagrams delivered (5.1).</td></tr>\n   873\t<tr><td><code>:max-candidates</code></td><td>4</td>\n   874\t<td>Candidates held per principal; the least sequence is dropped\n   875\t(5.5).</td></tr>\n   876\t<tr><td><code>:max-rejected</code></td><td>16</td>\n   877\t<td>Envelope ids of sequence-mismatched traces remembered per\n   878\tprincipal, oldest dropped; process state (5.5).</td></tr>\n   879\t<tr><td><code>:dao.stream.remote/resend-after</code></td><td>2</td>\n   880\t<td>The link's resend rule on UDP (<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code>\n   881\t2.5).</td></tr>\n   882\t<tr><td><code>:max-attachments</code></td><td>64</td>\n   883\t<td>Remote addresses the node's mirror holds channel state for;\n   884\tevict-oldest. An evicted asker's next request re-creates it. A memory\n   885\tbound only; it is not the exposure control (5.9).</td></tr>\n   886\t<tr><td>Head load retry</td>\n   887\t<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n   888\t<td>Doubling, as repair (5.5).</td></tr>\n   889\t<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n   890\t<td>A composition bound; more refuses startup.</td></tr>\n   891\t</table>\n   892\t\n   893\tFollowing never makes the node `busy?`. The owner's idle cadence must not\n   894\texceed `:head-poll-ticks`.\n   895\t\n   896\t## 8. What is rejected\n   897\t\n   898\t- **X1. The head under a key in the DHT** (a signed mutable record, as\n   899\t  BitTorrent's BEP 44). It survives the publisher going offline with no\n   900\t  relay configured, and that is its whole merit. Against it: it gives\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
