{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'parse-token|yin:|token|dht join|hydrated|hydration' test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc | head -55; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '30,89p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc | sed -n '246,280p'; rg -n 'no deadline|clock-free|deadline' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | head -15; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '135,149p'\"","aggregated_output":"test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:12:   answers the publisher's facts under the publisher's session token.\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:279:(def ^:private token-query\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:385:            (type! a token-query)\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:388:               token (second (re-find #\"\\\"([0-9a-f-]+)\\\"\" (str own)))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:393:              (is (some? token) (transcript a))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:394:              ;; Typed before the reader has hydrated: these lines wait in its\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:397:              (type! d token-query)\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:399:                (is (await-line d (re-pattern (str \"dht: hydrated :\" manifest))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:402:              (testing \"and q answers the publisher's fact under its token\"\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:406:                  (is (str/includes? (str answer) (str token)) (transcript d))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:407:                  (is (< (index-of-match d #\"dht: hydrated\")\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:412:                ;; Its require and q rounds each published over the hydrated\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:453:                    ;; D hydrated this very manifest at startup through the\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:480:                  (type! e token-query)\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:482:                    (is (await-line e (re-pattern (str \"dht: hydrated :\"\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:488:                      (is (str/includes? (str answer) (str token))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:939:                  (is (await-line p (re-pattern (str \"dht: hydrated :\"\ntest/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj:960:                (is (await-line c (re-pattern (str \"dht: hydrated :\" manifest))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:578:  \"The session token of every run that defined the literal 4242: the\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:594:            \"evaluation waits for the hydration, as it waits for recovery\")\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:597:                         #(line-with % \"hydrated\"))]\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:598:          (is (line-with lines (str \"hydrated \" manifest)))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:603:              \"the hydrated index is the reader directory's durable HEAD\")\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:606:            (is (str/includes? (str answer) (:shell-token pub))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:607:                (str \"q answers the publisher's fact, under its token: \"\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:609:            (is (not (str/includes? (str answer) (:shell-token reader)))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:629:            \"a refused hydration never admits evaluation over an empty index\")\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:667:              (do (is (some #{\"3\"} seen) \"evaluated once hydrated\")\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:668:                  (is (< (index-of seen (line-with seen \"hydrated\"))\ntest/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc:670:                      \"the hydration is reported before the first result\")\n    30\tand it changes nothing in how a name is resolved once an index is loaded.\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The owner's words\n    33\t\n    34\tVerbatim, 2026-10-05:\n    35\t\n    36\t> is it possible to start the yin.repl to always reference whatever the HEAD\n    37\t> is? (require 'alib) should always use the index manifest of the HEAD\n    38\t\n    39\t> publishing a's head is a what stigmergy is for. a can publish its head and\n    40\t> c can read it\n    41\t\n    42\tThe orchestrator's reading (a paraphrase, not the owner's words): the head\n    43\tis a trace A deposits in a shared medium and C perceives; it is not a\n    44\tmutable-pointer service C calls.\n    45\t\n    46\t**The reading holds.** No invariant contradicts it, and three require it\n    47\t(section 3: no server or client, single-writer provenance, a stream has no\n    48\tprivileged reader). One precision: across a network C's perception is\n    49\tcarried as the stream's own operations asked over a channel\n    50\t(`dao.stream.remote.md` section 2). Those are `cursor` and `next` on an\n    51\tordinary stream. No operation named after a head exists, and A's node\n    52\tanswers them without knowing what the stream holds.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. The invariants it rests on\n    55\t\n    56\t- **A host boundary is a stream boundary; a callback is an event on a\n    57\t  stream; a stream has no privileged reader and its meaning is not in it**\n    58\t  (`datom.world.md`, Streams and Host Boundaries). The head is a value on a\n    59\t  ring. The follower is one interpreter of it; a relay is another.\n    60\t- **Derive, don't persist** (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The\n    61\t  trace's sequence is the greatest transaction `t` of the index it names.\n    62\t  The publisher stores no counter and no trace.\n    63\t- **Sources stay separate db-values; union is explicit**\n    64\t  (`datom.world.md`, Tuples and Datoms). A followed index is never merged\n    65\t  into the reader's own index.\n    66\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics** (`datom.world.md`,\n    67\t  Architectural Commitments). The DHT keeps routing strict content\n    68\t  addresses and learns nothing about roots (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 1).\n    69\t- **Any stream may be exposed over WebSocket or UDP, peer to peer, through\n    70\t  a NAT; there is no server, no client and no privileged node**\n    71\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md` section 1, owner, 2026-09-25). The head ring is\n    72\t  one table entry; any node may serve one.\n    73\t- **A fetch has no deadline; liveness belongs to the drive through\n    74\t  `dao.lease`; the linker is clock-free** (owner, 2026-09-26;\n    75\t  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 8.3). A silent publisher ends nothing. Every delay\n    76\t  below is counted in the node's appended ticks, as its repair delays\n    77\t  already are.\n    78\t- **`dao.stream.apply` is independent of rpc** (owner, 2026-09-29). The\n    79\t  head trace is not an apply operation and adds no `:dao.stream.apply/*`\n    80\t  word. It uses a ring, the mirror and a reflection.\n    81\t- **Plain Clojure first; `yin.repl` uses the same path through host\n    82\t  functions** (owner, 2026-10-01; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 10, the\n    83\t  interface gate). Section 6 is that path.\n    84\t- **No backward compatibility** (owner). The three-part token and\n    85\t  a reader's hydration into HEAD are removed, not kept beside their\n    86\t  successors. What survives of hydration is the publisher's recovery,\n    87\t  under its own name (5.8).\n    88\t- **Single-writer provenance; the agent never addresses a recipient;\n    89\t  deposited traces are perceived by whoever observes** (`dao.agent.md`\n   246\t(defn request-get\n   247\t  \"Request one remote read of `address`. A non-segment address throws\n   248\t   before the wire. While an unsent request is owed the answer is\n   249\t   {:outcome :busy :state s} and nothing was submitted; otherwise the\n   250\t   outcome is returned with the minted id under :id, and the read's\n   251\t   completion arrives through step as {:id s :found? b :value v} (or an\n   252\t   :error, or a :lost tail).\"\n   253\t  [state address]\n   254\t  (segment-address! address)\n   255\t  (submit state {get-op address} false))\n   256\t\n   257\t\n   258\t(defn request-put\n   259\t  \"Request one remote put of `payload` at `address`. A non-segment\n   260\t   address throws before the wire; address-against-payload validation is\n   261\t   the server's at its own door, and a mismatch is the server's silence.\n   262\t   A plain put publishes :result :inserted or :result :present with no\n   263\t   verify read: the read-back is materialization's obligation.\"\n   264\t  [state address payload]\n   265\t  (segment-address! address)\n   266\t  (submit state {put-op address\n   267\t                 :jing/bytes (jing/bytes->base64\n   268\t                               (jing/canonical-bytes payload))}\n   269\t          false))\n   270\t\n   271\t\n   272\t(defn request-put-bytes\n   273\t  \"The bytes-level twin of request-put for a caller already holding the\n   274\t   canonical payload bytes -- the byte-store handle's own put. The bytes\n   275\t   must hash to `address`; the server's ingress check refuses them\n   276\t   otherwise, and a refused request is the server's silence.\"\n   277\t  [state address bs]\n   278\t  (segment-address! address)\n   279\t  (submit state {put-op address\n   280\t                 :jing/bytes (jing/bytes->base64 bs)}\n726:reason for that address (<code>/exhausted</code>, <code>/deadline</code>,\n1283:  or by the DHT's `ack-ticks` deadline. So each offered blob of each\n1873:deadline and remains deferred.\n2767:- Each of `/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo` and `/busy` reaches the\n   135\t(defn- fragment-envelope\n   136\t  [id part parts direction chunk]\n   137\t  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n   138\t   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n   139\t   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n   140\t   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n   141\t   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n   142\t\n   143\t\n   144\t(defn- fragments\n   145\t  [payload id direction]\n   146\t  (chunks/split payload max-datagram\n   147\t                (fn [part parts bytes]\n   148\t                  (fragment-envelope id part parts direction bytes))\n   149\t                cbor/encode))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-05 13:01:16 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 20:01:16 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a10c19-e77f-7631-b9d5-f26df7a7ff2f\n\nRead-only review completed. No files edited. “Resolved” below means resolved in the proposed design and acceptance criteria, not implemented.\n\n| Round-1 finding | Verdict | Document evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Sequence zero | **resolved** | Nil floor accepts zero: 418–446. H0/H2 test it: 1029–1035, 1138–1140. |\n| 2. Candidate snapshots | **resolved** | Explicit/installed holders determine snapshots; candidate-only loads are excluded: 523–529. H2 explicitly changes `snapshots`: 1125–1126; tests: 1142–1150. |\n| 3. Alias identity | **resolved** | Lookup names resolve to actual ring descriptors: 196–235. Contract amendments and identity tests: 1066–1104. |\n| 4. Poisoned floor | **partly** | Installed-only floor fixes rollback comparison: 417–460. Candidate scheduling can still exclude or block valid heads; findings below. |\n| 5. Crash ordering | **resolved** | Confirm → persist → install: 596–617. Crash/write-failure criteria: 1174–1181, 1215–1221. |\n| 6. Public reflector | **resolved for H0–H4** | Loopback restriction: 724–727; acceptance tests: 1105–1108, 1222–1223. Public serving remains contingent on separately reviewed H5. |\n| 7. Shared loads | **resolved in principle** | Per-consumer holders and selective release: 505–521. Shared-manifest tests: 1182–1188. Retry and compatibility details remain incomplete. |\n| 8. Publisher recovery | **resolved** | Explicit `--dht-recover`: 680–692. Governing-document amendment and recovery tests: 1233–1248. |\n\nThe revised snapshot rule correctly addresses the existing implementations: `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc:153–160` folds every loaded index, and `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1222–1227` currently supplies that unrestricted list.\n\nNew actionable findings:\n\n- **P1 | docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:468 | Candidate eviction still permits starvation.**  \n  **Evidence:** candidates retain unloadable traces during retries (490–496), but admission drops the least sequence above capacity (468–470; default capacity four at 873–875). Four unavailable signed high-sequence traces therefore cause a valid lower-sequence head to be discarded before it receives a load turn. This contradicts the claimed lower-loadable-head protection at 494–495 and 1158–1159. No key forgery is required to replay existing signed traces.  \n  **Concrete fix:** make retry-delayed candidates yield admission capacity to fresh candidates, with bounded fair scheduling. Specify holder release on eviction. Test four unavailable higher candidates followed by a lower, locally loadable valid head.\n\n- **P1 | docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:471 | An unfinished load can block newer heads.**  \n  **Evidence:** only one candidate per principal may load; no preemption or bounded concurrent alternative is specified. Release defers removal until the load ends (512–514). Existing code waits while fetching has no completion (`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1263–1266`). The proposal itself preserves deadline-free fetching (73–76). Consequently, the superseded-candidate criterion at 1160–1163 does not follow from the rules: head 12 cannot bypass an unfinished load of head 11.  \n  **Concrete fix:** specify bounded concurrent candidate loads or explicit preemption/detachment with bounded cleanup. Test a permanently unfinished old candidate while a newer head is entirely local; the newer head must install without terminating the old fetch by deadline.\n\n- **P2 | docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:490 | Retrying a failed shared load lacks a defined transition.**  \n  **Evidence:** unloadable candidates are scheduled again, but `dao.space.dht/load` preserves failed records (`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1157–1169`). Releasing/reacquiring the candidate holder cannot clear that record when another holder retains it. The revision specifies adding holders and removing records, but no retry operation that refreshes a failed shared record.  \n  **Concrete fix:** define an explicit failed-load restart transition preserving all holders, including its event semantics. Test failure, continued ownership by another consumer, subsequent blob availability, and successful follower retry.\n\n- **P2 | docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:1112 | H1 cannot satisfy its unchanged-test requirement.**  \n  **Evidence:** `forget` becomes release, including during loading (512–515). H1 requires every existing load test to pass unchanged (1112–1113), but `test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:721–727` explicitly requires `forget` during loading to throw `::dht/loading`.  \n  **Concrete fix:** either preserve `forget`’s loading refusal while allowing `release`, or explicitly migrate that test and document the changed contract in H1.\n\n- **P2 | docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:738 | H5’s cookie placement does not cover fragmented requests.**  \n  **Evidence:** the proposed cookie is an open key on the remote request, checked before channel allocation. UDP fragmentation replaces that request with envelopes containing only id, part, parts, direction, and bytes (`src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:135–149`); reassembly occurs before the whole request is deposited (`312–325`). The gate cannot inspect an inner request cookie before reassembly. H5 nevertheless requires gating fragments (1268–1271), while its expected files omit the UDP framing implementation and remote transport contract.  \n  **Concrete fix:** require the H5 design note to specify cookie-bearing outer fragment framing, pre-reassembly validation, and revised byte budgets. Add `udp.cljc` and `dao.stream.remote.md` to its scope. Keep public serving disabled until that review passes.\n\nN1’s volatile-follower relay prohibition is sound (618–622). N2’s loopback staging and later preallocation cookie gate are sound directions, subject to the H5 framing finding. N3 suppresses tokens on non-serving binds (693–697; test 1222–1223). N4 adds reset coverage (1046–1052), consistent with history carry-over in `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:222–233`.\n\nThe new lookup and holder mechanisms introduce no inherent violation of apply/rpc independence, clock-free linking, principal authority, or separate db-values. Persisting signed observations supplies evidence unavailable from the reader’s own index. H5 may amend the frozen DHT contract through the separately reviewed process it explicitly requires; “frozen” is accurately cited from `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:3`. The amendment must keep head semantics outside the DHT.\n\nThe extra detail mostly repairs necessary correctness gaps. Automatic relaying, multiple sources, and public transport hardening expand the initial delivery scope. A simpler sound first milestone is one explicitly configured publisher source, durable installed-head persistence, isolated candidate loads, and loopback REPL following; defer automatic relaying and multi-source scheduling. Cross-machine use still requires reviewed transport hardening.\n\nBlocked from adoption by candidate starvation and unfinished-load blocking; also resolve shared-load retry semantics, H1’s contradictory test criterion, and H5’s fragment-gating scope."}}
